    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/project-manager-6055036": {
      "title": "Project Manager · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Project Manager · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Project Manager Project Manager Back to jobs Project Manager Job details Posted 08 October 2026 Salary £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 210334 Expiry 28 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose This role represents an exciting opportunity to provide high level and specialist project management and administrative service to the ‘Delivering Humanised Places’ project funded by UKRI. Based in the Division of Urban Studies and Social Policy, the project will involve working with an interdisciplinary team within the University of Glasgow as well as in partnership with Heatherwick Studio/Humanise. The role will involve coordinating and running meetings and workshops with a wide range of stakeholders and communities to generate insights that will inform project, with the support and input of the larger, interdisciplinary research team. The postholder will be expected to develop relationships with policy actors from different policy sectors, as well as key public and private sector stakeholders. The successful candidate will have significant experience in providing a full range of project management functions, including stakeholder management, higher-level administrative services and multi-site team coordination for higher education, government and/or Third Sector projects involving co-production and participatory approaches. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work independently and as part of a team are requirements for the job. Main Duties and Responsibilities Take a lead role, in collaboration with the research team to plan, monitor and review the strategic aims, objectives and key milestones of the Delivering Humanised Places project. Plan and coordinate a series of activities, meetings and workshops with diverse groups of participants from multiple policy sectors. Manage the logistics of the workshops, coordinating the facilitators and managing all data and materials that are required or generated during these events. Managing internal and external relationships relevant to the project. Engage in strategic business planning processes and the management of project work by providing specialist administrative support to the research team and supplying relevant information and statistical analysis as required. Establish and sustain project management processes and key project documents (e.g. report writing, risk logs) to ensure a record of contacts, events, outputs, and other project activities are kept up-to-date, accessible and accurate. Take a key role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues in the Division, University, Heatherwick Studio/Humanise, as well as the wider academic, industry, policy, and stakeholder community. Plan, promote and manage logistics for virtual and in-person meetings and events and other research and engagement activities run by the project, instigating and monitoring appropriate follow-up actions to ensure quality enhancement. Proactively identify opportunities to enhance the reach of the project, for example presenting work at local, national or international conferences, seminars, meetings, colloquia and workshops to contribute to knowledge exchange and develop and enhance the profile of the project. Lead on the development of a Communication Strategy with Heatherwick Studio/Humanise, for example setting up and maintain the project’s web and social media presence producing briefings, blogs, podcasts, explainer videos and similar material,  and providing promotional content to other channels such as the University’s Marketing teams, as required by the research team. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by Line Manager or Unit Director. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan, World Changers Together. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 Either Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 9 (Ordinary Degree, Scottish Vocational Qualification level 4), or equivalent (including professional accreditation with relevant formal training), and experience of personal development in a similar or related role(s),. Or: Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary  knowledge and skills in a similar or number of different roles. A2. Excellent knowledge of a range of project management tools and approaches that can be used to support higher education-led research projects. A3 Knowledge and understanding of the UK local authority sector. A4 Excellent knowledge and understanding of project and event planning and coordination. Desirable B1 Some familiarity with environmental sustainability issues and/or related social and political contexts. B2. Formal programme management qualification, e.g. PRINCE2 (or working towards this). Skills Essential C1 Excellent verbal and written communication skills, and ability to communicate effectively with diverse technical and lay audiences using a range of media. C2 Excellent interpersonal skills, including the ability to effectively communicate, work with and engage with researchers, policy makers, members of the public and community groups. C3 Proven analytical, influencing and problem-solving capability. C4 Advanced IT skills, including using online platforms to host events, workshops, or interactive sessions, report and presentation writing, and skills for web editing using a content management system. C5 Proven skills with using social media to promote projects and engage stakeholders and the wider public. C6 Ability to work flexibly and adapt to changing environments. C7 Ability to work across sectoral boundaries with a wide range of professions and disciplines representing academia, local authorities and other sectors relevant to the project. Experience Essential E1 Experience of all aspects of research communication and events organisation. E2 Experience of dealing with processes and procedures for partnership working and public engagement. E3. Experience of  planning, co-ordinating, monitoring and recording of research activities and their impact. Desirable F1 Experience of workshop facilitation. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum pro rata. This post is part time (21 hours per week) and fixed term for up to 15 months or fixed till 31.03.2028 Closing date: 23:45 28th October 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Project Manager Glasgow Posted 4 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/mep-operations-manager-6054881": {
      "title": "MEP Operations Manager · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "MEP Operations Manager · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs MEP Operations Manager MEP Operations Manager Back to jobs MEP Operations Manager Job details Posted 08 October 2026 Salary Grade 8, £50,253 - £58,225 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 208833 Expiry 05 Nov 2026 22:45 Job description Job Purpose The post holder is responsible for the management of allocated human, financial and physical resources, providing effective management to the Mechanical, Electrical and Plumbing (MEP) Operations teams, equipping and enabling them to deliver efficient, high-quality buildings, spaces and places, ensuring maximum availability and useability of the same. The post holder shall embrace digital technologies with a strong drive towards sustainability, supporting the development of smart buildings for the benefit of users, and the environment. The post holder will contribute to the delivery of the department’s strategic and operational targets, overall service delivery aims and be accountable for implementation and delivery of all relevant policy, objectives and standards. This involves the review, development and implementation of continuous improvements in working initiatives and working patterns to ensure value-for-money arrangements which are customer focused, tailored to the needs of stakeholders and which fulfil departmental and University strategies and objectives. The post holder will ensure suitable professional and technical development of the internal operations/trades teams’ competency and capability to support the operations of the existing Estate and the future complex buildings that will be delivered as part of the University’s Campus Development Plan. Main Duties and Responsibilities Leadership and Management To effectively manage the mechanical electrical and plumbing trades groups ensuring the delivery of the day-to-day operation. Responsible for leadership of operational staff including, but not limited to, lead technicians, supervisors, technicians, technical assistants, apprentices and nominated operatives. Establish performance indicators coupled to a training matrix to ensure best means of monitoring and developing yourself and your team. To support the head of Technical Services with the development and delivery of strategic and tactical goals and objectives, to permeate throughout the MEP teams, which align with departmental and wider University operating plans. To work with other Operations Managers, individuals, and teams, across Technical Services and Facilities Services to oversee the service delivery of a reactive maintenance and emergency service to meet ‘Business as Usual’ service obligations to the University. Develop an Incident response/ incident management template and framework. Establish and maintain an on-call service for out of hour’s operations and escalations. To lead all direct reports in planning scheduling and reviewing workloads and budgets to ensure timeous and cost-effective achievement of service delivery output targets. To manage delegated budgets and ensure good governance, financial control and value for money. To manage and operate relevant HR procedures in relation to recruitment and selection, performance and development, managing attendance, and grievance/disciplinary in consultation with Human Resources. To ensure adequate reporting mechanisms and templates to allow both you and your teams to give a clear indication of related MEP performance and common goals of maximum up-time and Customer satisfaction. Technical Expertise To provide expertise, guidance and leading advice to colleagues on all aspects of the operation of MEP installations/Building services, including statutory compliance, planned and reactive maintenance. To focus the Technical Operations Team in respect of developing expertise in the proactive operation and monitoring of complex plant and building systems ensuring available skills to undertake first response and to ensure appropriate localised corrective action is taken, in order to return buildings, spaces and places to operational and useable condition. Instil an ethos of safe working practices, safe systems of work, risk awareness and management.  Give careful attention to environmental considerations within our areas of technical expertise and operations. Maintain close working relationships with our digital and sustainability colleagues and teams, to ensure we align our technical expertise with innovative, smart and sustainable solutions. Change management and Continuous Improvement To work with the Head of Technical Services to embed a culture where new ideas and innovation are encouraged and opportunities created, trialling new ways of working and developing a process of continuous improvement. To lead a review and improvement of work practices and policies; Review and develop the team to ensure the service has the necessary resource, competence, skills, knowledge, plant and equipment to achieve agreed targets and to provide continuous and sustainable professional Services to the University. Customer service To work collaboratively with all colleagues, supporting the development and implementation of an improved Customer Service strategy, ensuring direct contact with key stakeholders, promoting improvements in service delivery, ensuring fit-for-purpose buildings, spaces and places. Soft landings To provide specialist technical input into construction projects, influencing designs and specifications to ensure consistent standards are achieved. To work with the Programme Manager and colleagues in the Construction and Project Management Team in the adoption of BSRIA Soft Landings or similar industry standard methodology/framework. Provide feedback to ensure an effective lessons learned approach is adopted to ensure continuous improvement in project delivery. To support the Head of Technical Services, and other colleagues, in accepting handover of relevant projects, to ensure necessary compliance certification is received and training is undertaken by key operations staff at handover including a review of completed Operations and Maintenance and Health and Safety files. Maintenance Management To apply professional specialist expertise and judgement to oversee a quality service to required key performance standards. To be responsible for supporting regular updates to asset registers/condition survey database and FMR (Forward Maintenance Register) providing leading input to programmes of work for planned and preventative maintenance. To plan and organise individual and team activities, ensuring plans and actions complement the strategic capital and maintenance plans. To develop business case proposals for replacement of obsolete/life-expired and failed plant and equipment, recommending key areas of focus. Compliance and Risk Management To be responsible and accountable for the to day operations of systems, plant and equipment. Lead on the permit to work arrangements for critical systems e.g. gas, fire alarms, electrical systems etc. to control shutdowns, isolations and the coordination or overlapping contractor activity. To be responsible and accountable for compliance standards and ensure that relevant areas of responsibility are maintained and operated in compliance with such agreed standards across the University Estate To identify risks, implement risk reduction and control measures and support business continuity plans to provide reliability and resilience of all services. To develop and implement safety management arrangements including risk assessments and method statement for complex technical tasks. Supplier/Contractor Management To provide leading support to Framework and Contracts colleagues, satisfying such requirements as, but not limited to, initial technical investigations, spot-check audits and summary reporting of findings. Work collaboratively in support of, and directly with, our Service Partners. To provide detailed feedback to Frameworks and Contracts, and other relevant functional areas, with regards to contractor performance for inclusion on relevant KPI measures. Governance and Reporting To ensure document and information management protocols are strictly applied with document retention arrangements appropriate to relevant legislative and University requirements. Provide informational reports and documentation as necessary to the function. Other To deputise for the Head of Technical Services where required. To provide leading operational support to other University Services for key UoG, VIP and Corporate events e.g. open days, graduations, visitations, etc. Collaboratively support our UoG Service desk to enhance the Customer experience across Campuses, buildings, spaces and places. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and ExperienceKnowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary professional knowledge and management skills in a similar or number of different specialist roles. Or, Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 9, 10 or 11 (Ordinary/Honours Degree, Post Graduate Qualification in Engineering/Building Services or related discipline), or equivalent, including being professionally qualified in relevant discipline, with a broad range of professional experience in a management role(s). A2 Evidence of Continuing Professional Development relevant to role. A3 High level of knowledge of the key Building Services/MEP maintenance and legislative requirement priorities in relation to a complex estate. A4 Extensive knowledge of Fire, Security and Building Management Control systems. A5 Thorough practical knowledge of the content and application of Health and Safety legislation within the construction industry. A6 Working knowledge of HR processes including recruitment and selection, performance management, training and development, workforce planning, discipline and grievance. A7 Knowledge of principles of BSRIA Soft landings. Desirable: B1 IOSH Managing Safely or CITB Site Safety Management qualification. B2 Chartered Membership of a relevant professional body. B3 Knowledge of standard forms of construction contract. B4 Knowledge of SFG20 specifications. B5 Knowledge of Higher Education and Public Sector Estates Management guidance. Skills Essential: C1 Proven management capability and well-developed skills in managing large staff groups with a demonstrable ability to effect positive changes in work practices. C2 Strong written and verbal communication skills with the ability to interact and liaise effectively, clearly explain matters of detail, listen accurately and discuss matters of detail with specialist and non-specialists. C3 Excellent planning, organisational and time management skills with the ability to understand work requirements, work to deadlines, prioritise and operate effectively under pressure. C4 Effective interpersonal and team-working skills with the ability to quickly develop and maintain effective relationships and contribute positively within a range of teams. C5 Pro-active with the ability to use initiative and sound judgement to resolve issues independently. C6 Well-developed IT skills including use of standard desktop applications such as Microsoft Word, Excel and Outlook and with the capability to develop further IT skills to utilise specialist IT applications. C7 Proven ability to develop staff through talent spotting and pro-active staff development plans. C8 Proven analytical and problem-solving capability with the ability to prioritise complex and competing tasks. C9 Proven developed skills in managing budgets within agreed cashflows. Experience Essential: E1 Significant relevant experience as a professional Building Services Engineering practitioner. E2 Significant, relevant and recent experience in the management of supervisory and operational teams of staff within a similar organisation. E3 Experience of providing advice to Management and Leadership teams in the direction and delivery of the maintenance and replacement programmes. E4 Experience of delivering operational engineering/building services within a complex estate with a similar range of historic and modern architecture including listed properties and conservation areas. E5 Experience in safety management; developing and implementing safety plans with risk reduction and control principles. E6 Recent experience of implementing continuous improvement programmes which lead to ongoing business and service delivery improvement. Desirable: F1 Experience of using CAFM systems to schedule maintenance tasks, update and upload reports on planned maintenance activities. F2 Experience in overseeing the operation and maintenance of HV networks. F3 Experience of using Trend/Distech BMS systems. F4 Experience of the operation of a District Heating Scheme including CHP engine. F5 Demonstrable experience of the management of staff and contractors. F6 Experience of working within Higher Education or an alternative analogous sector such as the NHS. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 8, £50,253 - £58,225 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours p/w) and open ended. Relocation assistance will be provided where appropriate. Closing date: 5 November 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job MEP Operations Manager Glasgow Posted 4 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/administrative-assistant-5976858": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Administrative Assistant Administrative Assistant Back to jobs Administrative Assistant Job details Posted 07 October 2026 Salary Grade 5:  £28,031 - £31,236 per annum End date 18 November 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 204913 Expiry 18 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To provide administrative support within the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences (MVLS) Shared Research Facilities (SRF) and Biological Safety Team. The postholder will be a point-of-contact for users of the SRF and be responsible for coordinating responses to enquiries, providing quotes and financial tracking of projects. The postholder will also assist in biological safety management tasks including collation of biosafety data required for monitoring and audit purposes and have clerking responsibilities for Genetic Modification Safety Committee (GMSC) meetings. This role requires a high degree of organisation to ensure that administrative tasks are dealt with effectively and the postholder will need to develop successful working relationships with academic, technical and professional services colleagues. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1.  Direct enquiries to the appropriate SRF Technical Leads efficiently, providing requested information and quotes promptly and on-time and managing effective communication with users. 2.  Be responsible for supporting grant submissions for SRF users, liaising with SRF Technical Leads and grant managers to ensure costs are appropriately included and approved in accordance with internal and external deadlines. 3.  Be responsible for efficient financial administration of SRF projects including accurate tracking and filing of projects, management of administrative databases, timely processing of charges. 4.  Provide administrative support and act as clerk for SRF operational meetings ensuring that accurate minutes are kept and distributed and supporting follow‑up actions. 5.  Assist in the organisation of SRF training courses including preparing schedules, advertising courses, managing course registrations and payments, liaising with staff delivering the courses to ensure course materials are available on Moodle and compiling feedback reports from course attendees. 6.  Provide administration support for Biological Safety management tasks including the monitoring and responding to e-mail enquiries, logging and filling of GM risk assessments and recording outcomes of GM risk assessments reviews. 7.  Assist in collation of biosafety data required for audit purposes by the Safety and Environmental Protection Service (SEPS) and external regulatory bodies. 8.  Act as clerk for Genetic Modification Safety Committee meetings. This will include the booking venues, arranging Teams links, preparation and circulation of agendas and papers. 9.  Assist in the organisation and delivery of SRF and Biological Safety workshops and other events. This includes room bookings, managing, planning, and booking catering, travel and accommodation where necessary. 10.  Assist SRF and Biological Safety marketing and communications initiatives by preparing regular internal updates and by supporting the development of newsletters and social media content. 11.  Engage in reasonable professional development activities as required. 12.  Conduct such other duties temporarily or on a continuing basis, as may reasonably be required, commensurate with your grade. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge and Qualifications Essential: A1 Higher National Diploma, Scottish Vocational Qualification Level 3, Higher or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 Knowledge of administrative and support systems, process design and improvement, and developing, interrogating and maintaining databases. Desirable: B1 Knowledge and understanding of higher education policies, practices and procedures. Skills Essential: C1 Excellent organisational and time management skills with ability to work flexibly, manage own workload effectively and prioritise demands to ensure that deadlines are met. C2 Ability to prepare written reports, collate and summarise information effectively to a range of stakeholders. C3 Ability to understand financial information. C4 Excellent IT skills including the use of Microsoft packages (e.g. Word and Excel), Zoom, Teams, Outlook and databases. C5 Excellent attention to detail, ensuring accuracy in all actions. C6 Ability to coordinate a range of activities and forward plan. C7 Ability to use initiative, multi-task, and resolve problems independently and/or through a support team. C8 Excellent written and oral communication skills. C9 Ability to cultivate, build and maintain excellent relationships. C10 Excellent interpersonal skills and strong customer service orientation using tact, diplomacy, discretion and confidentiality as appropriate. Desirable: D1 Competency in University management systems e.g. Agresso, Ivanti, MyCampus and Moodle. Experience Essential: E1 Relevant administrative experience gained through relevant work experience in a service delivery environment. E2 Experience of dealing effectively with a wide range of customers and staff at all levels in an organisation, using initiative and judgement to proactively resolve issues. E3 Experience of processing, analysing and presenting complex information. E4 Experience of clerking meetings and providing accurate minutes of meetings. Desirable: F1 Experience of producing newsletters, e-bulletins and publications or similar. F2 Proven track record of financial and project management. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and open ended (permanent). As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  18 November 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Administrative Assistant Glasgow Posted 5 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
