    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/graduate-teaching-assistant-school-of-geographical-and-earth-sciences-6045038": {
      "title": "Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Geographical & Earth Sciences Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Geographical & Earth Sciences Back to jobs Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Geographical & Earth Sciences Job details Posted 01 October 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type GTA and Demonstrator Reference 209773 Expiry 31 Mar 2027 23:45 Job description Applications for this post will only be processed where you are a University of Glasgow student and have been given the vacancy number by your Local School Administrator. Job Purpose Within a clear and established teaching programme, contribute to the delivery of an excellent student experience by undertaking a range of teaching, assessment and administrative duties, principally at undergraduate level, to further the understanding of key course topics and assist students in drawing out key learning points from course materials. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Contribute to the planning, organisation and delivery of undergraduate teaching. 2. Deliver a range of teaching and assessment activities directed towards the delivery of subjects at undergraduate level. 3. Participate in a range of course administration duties, within required timescales, including effective communication of information, marking, assessment and timeous constructive feedback. 4. Assist with the development of appropriate teaching materials ensuring content and methods of delivery meet determined learning objectives. 5. Contribute to the effective use of learning technologies to support and enhance course delivery, course organisation, feedback and assessment. This may involve blended and/or online provision including the use of Moodle and other IT materials. 6. Apply specialist knowledge to teaching that best meet the needs of individuals and groups of learners, ensuring an inclusive and evidence-based approach that promotes student participation and learning outcome attainment. 7. Participate in the full assessment process using a variety of methods and techniques, including oral assessment, and provide effective, timely and appropriate feedback to students that supports their learning. 8. Assist with the supervision of student projects, dissertations, and any practical work, advising on skills, methods and techniques to assist the transfer of knowledge, and respond appropriately to the diverse range of learner support/ needs. 9. Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 10. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School. 11. To contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan. Qualifications Honours degree (SCQF Level 10) relevant to the teaching area or have expertise in a relevant field, together with an understanding of the principles of teaching, learning and assessment. Registered for and working towards the achievement of a PhD (MPhil/PhD) or PGT qualification in a relevant subject. Knowledge, Skills and Experience Expert knowledge of the subject area. Experience of delivering and supporting undergraduate or postgraduate students either in an online, distance learning or face to face environment. Experience of supervising, mentoring or teaching students in practical and active learning environments. Relevant administrative experience (e.g. student support, course administration etc.) Excellent communication and presentation skills. Ability to use IT and relevant software packages to support teaching and learning. Proven ability to work independently and as part of a team. Ability to work to deadlines. Commitment to the University’s published values and professional behaviours. Apply Now Save this job Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Geographical & Earth Sciences Glasgow Posted 11 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/systems-administrator-6044048": {
      "title": "Systems Administrator · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Systems Administrator · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Systems Administrator Systems Administrator Back to jobs Systems Administrator Job details Posted 30 September 2026 Salary Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum Location SUERC Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 209233 Expiry 28 Oct 2026 22:45 Job description Job Purpose The Systems Administrator plays a key role in ensuring the availability, performance, and security of IT systems supporting the Scottish Universities Environmental Research Centre (SUERC). Based primarily at the SUERC site, the postholder will provide operational support for 90-100 staff, delivering administration, maintenance, and support of local server infrastructure, networks, end-user devices, websites, collaboration platforms, and specialist systems that underpin SUERC's research, operational, and administrative activities. Reporting locally to the Head of Operations, with support and engagement from the University of Glasgow College IT function and Central IS team, the postholder will work closely with both local stakeholders and central University IT teams to maintain reliable services and coordinate infrastructure improvements, upgrades, and projects. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1 Contribute to the installation, configuration, maintenance, and support of server infrastructure, including physical and virtual servers, storage systems, cloud services, and network components that support SUERC operations. 2 Monitor system performance, investigate faults, and proactively address issues to ensure high levels of service availability, resilience, and performance. 3 Troubleshoot and resolve a broad range of technical issues affecting infrastructure, network services, AV equipment, specialist research systems, end-user devices, and associated hardware. 4 Provide first and second-line technical support to SUERC staff, ensuring incidents, service requests, and technical enquiries are resolved in a timely and professional manner. 5 Administer and maintain SUERC's inventory of IT assets, including laptops, desktops, Apple Mac devices, peripherals, and associated equipment, ensuring asset records remain accurate and up to date. 6 Coordinate the procurement, deployment, replacement, and lifecycle management of IT equipment in accordance with university policies and local operational requirements. 7 Provide administration and user support for SUERC SharePoint sites, document libraries, permissions, and associated Microsoft 365 collaboration tools, ensuring effective information management and access controls. 8 Support the maintenance, development, and administration of SUERC websites, including content management systems such as WordPress, coordinating with stakeholders and third-party suppliers where required. 9 Act as a liaison between SUERC and the University of Glasgow Information Services and College IT teams, coordinating infrastructure projects, network upgrades, systems enhancements, and service improvements. 10 Support the planning and delivery of major IT-related projects, including network communications upgrades, server refresh programmes, systems migrations, and infrastructure improvements. 11 Assist with the administration, monitoring, testing, and documentation of backup, disaster recovery, and business continuity arrangements to ensure organisational resilience. 12 Conduct regular testing of backup and recovery processes and maintain associated documentation to support SUERC's Business Continuity Planning (BCP) requirements. 13 Document system configurations, procedures, support activities, technical standards, and operating procedures to facilitate knowledge sharing, operational continuity, and audit compliance. 14 Work collaboratively with University IT specialists, external vendors, contractors, and technical stakeholders to support service delivery and project implementation. 15 Follow all agreed operating policies, procedures, security standards, and practices as approved by University Information Services, College IT teams, and SUERC management to maintain a secure and robust environment. 16 Undertake any other duties and projects appropriate to the grade as required by the Head of Operations or College IT management. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge, Qualifications Either: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post through relevant knowledge, skills, and experience gained in a similar role. Or Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework Level 7 (Advanced Higher, Scottish Vocational Qualification Level 3, Higher National Certificate) or equivalent, together with experience of personal development in a similar technical role. A2 Technical knowledge of server, network, and systems administration concepts. A3 Working knowledge of information security good practice within a complex networked environment. A4 Understanding of technical requirements and the ability to translate user needs into practical solutions. Desirable: B1 Knowledge of system monitoring, capacity planning, and infrastructure performance management. B2 Awareness of Cyber Essentials Plus, ISO 27001, and related information security frameworks. Skills Essential: C1 Strong problem-solving and troubleshooting skills, with the ability to investigate technical issues, identify root causes, and implement effective solutions. C2 Ability to manage and maintain accurate system, asset, and technical documentation. C3 Ability to work independently while recognising when issues require escalation to senior technical colleagues. C4 Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively with technical and non-technical stakeholders. C5 Ability to adapt to evolving technologies, systems, and organisational requirements. Experience Essential: E1 Experience supporting end-user computing devices, including Windows and Apple Mac computers / OS, laptops, peripherals, printers, and mobile devices. E2 Experience using scripting and automation tools such as Windows PowerShell and/or Linux shell scripting. E3 Experience in troubleshooting and supporting Microsoft 365 services, including SharePoint Online and associated collaboration tools. Desirable: F1 Experience working within a research, higher education, scientific, or laboratory environment. F2 Experience supporting specialist scientific equipment, laboratory-connected systems, data acquisition platforms, or research computing environments. F3 Experience coordinating with centralised IT service providers within a large organisation. F4 Experience supporting and configuring specialist software applications. F5 Experience supporting virtualised and networked environments, including storage systems and backup technologies. F6 Experience supporting content management systems, preferably WordPress, including user administration, maintenance, troubleshooting, and system support. F7 Experience coordinating infrastructure upgrades, migrations, and service improvement projects involving multiple stakeholders. F8 Experience working to and with business continuity processes. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours p/w) and open ended. Closing date: 28 October 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Systems Administrator Posted 12 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/network-manager-project-manager-6042588": {
      "title": "Network Manager (Project Manager) · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Network Manager (Project Manager) · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Network Manager (Project Manager) Network Manager (Project Manager) Back to jobs Network Manager (Project Manager) Job details Posted 29 September 2026 Salary Grade 8, £50,253 - £58,225 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 209114 Expiry 20 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose The “EPSRC Responsible and Resilient Electronic and Electromagnetic (RaRE) Materials Network+” is a major UKRI investment towards building a UK-wide community connecting academics, industry, and the public/policy. RARE Materials Network+ will be the focal point for creating a sustainable flow for translating material innovation to electronic systems. We will connect the material science and development community to end-users and innovators in electronics across telecommunications, quantum technologies, and power electronics. The Network+ will foster a more unified community and adopt transferable, industry-standard characterisation across materials’ electronic and electromagnetic performance, which enable sector-agnostic innovation. We will also allocate significant funding to the community through pump-priming projects and to facilitate career development. The postholder will manage the overall delivery and operation of the RaRE Materials Network+, playing a leading role in championing the Network, building productive relationships with stakeholders, creating opportunities for collaboration, and strengthening the Network’s profile across the UK. You will lead stakeholder engagement and business development activities, identifying and securing new partnerships and collaborative opportunities to support the successful delivery of the Network’s strategic aims.   You will engage with potential users of the research and facilitate dialogue with industry on technological challenges and translational opportunities, helping to maximise the impact of the Network’s activities. The postholder will provide comprehensive support to the PI and Co-Is, including supporting governance, financial monitoring, risk management, reporting, research dissemination and strategic planning, ensuring that the project stays on track and that objectives, milestones and deliverables, as well as any changes in the research, are documented. You will represent the Network at conferences, industry events, trade shows and government/parliamentary events, helping to enhance its visibility, influence and impact across academia, industry and the wider stakeholder community. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. To manage and coordinate the overall delivery and operation of the Network, ensuring effective communication of decisions, priorities and outcomes to key stakeholders. 2. To provide comprehensive project management support to the Principal Investigator and co-PIs, including planning and monitoring, financial management, logistical support, change management, reporting, and coordination of funds allocated to WPs. Collaborate closely with Project staff to ensure efficient project planning, resourcing, and delivery. 3. To communicate with all relevant partners and stakeholder communities, including consultation and management of expectations and engaging and managing stakeholders from senior management to operational staff to ensure clarity in the delivery of key programme messages (verbal and written). 4. Lead on the development and implementation of comprehensive marketing and business engagement strategies utilising market insights and analysis to fuel business growth. 5. Lead the planning and delivery of Network events, including workshops and sandpits, working closely with the theme leads and special interest groups. 6. Contribute to the formulation and dissemination of the Flexible funding including formulating funding calls, overseeing the independent review process, and supporting the funded project leads. 7. Represent the Network including at exhibitions and industrial tradeshows, technical conferences, and other events, in the UK and internationally. 8. Lead on developing and securing a mature pipeline of strategically relevant contracts/collaborations with SMEs, academia and industry and enhance direct commercial funding in support of the Network’s long-term sustainability. 9. Initiate and lead business attraction and bid initiatives to seize new opportunities. 10. Proactively build and maintain effective relationships with key partners and stakeholders, identifying opportunities and facilitating collaborations for new projects 11. Effectively support the Network’s members with research and dissemination including whitepapers, press releases, and non-technical briefs. 12. Act as the key contact coordinating any requests from funders for periodic updates on the grant progress, visits and requests for information, audits or to attend events or activities organised by funders concerning the programme. 13. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the PI. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary professional knowledge and management skills in a similar or number of different specialist roles. Or: Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 9,10 or 11 (Ordinary/Honours Degree, Post Graduate Qualification), or equivalent, including being professionally qualified in relevant discipline, with a broad range of professional experience in a management role(s). A2 Knowledge of project management techniques and tools. A3 Knowledge of the Higher Education organisations, and their purpose, infrastructure, policies and contractual legislation. A4 A thorough understanding of grant management processes. A5 Good knowledge of financial management, processes and systems. A6 Knowledge of effective communication, publicity, and dissemination skills and approaches. A7 Knowledge and understanding of data integrity and its importance. A8 A strong understanding of risk management. Desirable: B1 Detailed knowledge of UKRI funding schemes, their rules and their processes. B2 Degree or equivalent research/industrial experience in the electronics sector, preferably around materials. Skills Essential: C1 Excellent project management skills – both short-term deadline driven and longer term involving multiple conflicting deadlines and coordination across multiple organisational units. C2 Excellent time management skills with an ability to prioritise competing demands and multiple projects. C3 Proven exceptional verbal and written communication and organisational skills to produce clear project management documents and stakeholder focused content, presented in an engaging and constructive manner. C4 Financial management and planning skills. C5 Ability to provide advice and guidance in relation to funder requirements. C6 Ability to build and manage business relationships bridging between higher education and partners from industry and charity organisations. Experience Essential: E1 Track record in electronics/tech and/or materials. E2 Experience of managing a portfolio of external engagement activities and projects across the full lifecycle with a successful track record of delivery. E3 Experience of establishing, developing and maintaining relevant marketing intelligence and knowledge systems and utilising them effectively in business development and customer support. E4 Experience of attending partnering meetings and hosting company visits. E5 Experience and knowledge of working and collaborating with electronics/circular economy or similar companies and understanding of their business drivers. Desirable: F1 Experience of working in the electronics or (electronic) materials industry. F2 Experienced in allocating funding through competitive process, such as through grant funders, investment funds, or in R&D management. F3 Experience of providing high-quality advice and guidance on research management. F4 Demonstrable effective networking experience including community building through working groups, societies, or other membership/community-based organisations. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 8, £50,253 - £58,225 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours p/w) and has funding to 31 March 2030. Relocation assistance may be provided where appropriate. Closing date: 20 October 2026 All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Network Manager (Project Manager) Glasgow Posted 13 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
