    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/clinical-university-lecturer-in-adult-outreach-dentistry-6061250": {
      "title": "Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry",
      "description": "Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Back to jobs Featured Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Job details Posted 15 October 2026 Salary Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Clinical Reference 208333 Expiry 12 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description We are pleased to offer an exciting opportunity for a Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry with a focus on Adult Dental Outreach to join the Glasgow Dental School, part of the School of Medicine, Dentistry and Nursing, within the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences (MVLS) at the University of Glasgow. As one of the largest Dental Schools in the UK and the largest in Scotland, Glasgow Dental School is consistently ranked among the top Dental Schools nationwide. Our school is committed to delivering world-leading, student-centred learning and teaching using modern and dynamic approaches to education. We are proud of our vibrant Community of Dental Educational Research and Scholarship Group, which drives innovation and leadership in dental education on a global scale. Our Bachelor of Dental Surgery (BDS) programme is a modern, integrated five-year programme with an undergraduate study population of approximately 450-500. The programme has achieved excellent rankings in recent national league tables. Our advanced and collaborative curriculum recognises, respects, and fully supports individuals, to flourish and become a world changer. In partnership with the NHS, the undergraduate programme provides outstanding opportunities for clinical training in adult outreach facilities. The function of this post is to provide clinical teaching and student supervision in our partner Greater Glasgow & Clyde (GGC) Health Board adult outreach clinics. You will also provide NHS clinical sessions at the level of a specialty dentist in Adult Dentistry within the GGC Public Dental Service. The University of Glasgow Dental School also offers postgraduate research opportunities and taught postgraduate programmes in a range of subjects. Our students and staff benefit from cutting-edge facilities supporting patient care, clinical training, education and research. The Dental School is home to two internationally recognised research groups in Oral Sciences and Community Oral Health, which collaborate with international research partners. In partnership with NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde, we operate an active Dental Clinical Research Facility. We welcome applications from candidates with a registrable qualification with the General Dental Council and significant clinical experience in Adult Dentistry. Job Purpose To deliver undergraduate education and carry out clinical duties in Adult Outreach Dentistry, including the development of innovative approaches to teaching and educational scholarship. This will take place mainly in the Adult Outreach Dentistry setting. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. To play a key role in the delivery of teaching of Adult Outreach Dentistry to undergraduate dental students (up to 100 per year) with a focus on teaching and coordination of the Adult Outreach programme. This includes logistics and enhancing student experience in relation to accessing Primary Care adult patients within the GGC health board area. The role requires close collaboration with the Public Dental Service, its managerial and clinical staff and its processes. 2. To participate in the design and delivery of a range of teaching and assessment activities within Adult Outreach Dentistry in Glasgow Dental Hospital C School and Adult Outreach for undergraduate students, in accordance with the teaching objectives of the Dental School and the intended learning outcomes of the General Dental Council. This would include delivery of tutorials and potentially lectures in addition to clinical teaching. 3. To participate in the development of creative and innovative approaches to teaching and learning, ensuring that the educational opportunities available to students are maintained and significantly improved. 4. To provide effective, timely and appropriate feedback to students to support learning. 5. To undertake administrative duties associated with the delivery of undergraduate teaching and assessment. 6. To undertake the clinical duties associated with the post as appropriate to an Honorary Specialty Dentist in Adult Outreach Dentistry. Personal clinical duties will increase during non-term/non annual leave time. These clinical duties will address the current needs of the NHS Public Dental with a focus on areas that would be beneficial to recruitment of suitable patients to the Adult Dental Outreach Centres within NHSGGC. 7. To undertake assessment activities (including OSCE and written papers) within exam diets. 8. To provide clinical teaching and assessment within Glasgow Dental Hospital and School, especially out with Outreach teaching periods. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1. Registrable qualification with the General Dental Council. A2. Must be fully registered with the GDC at the time of post commencement. Desirable: B1. Fellowship in Dental Surgery, MFDS from one of the Royal Surgical Colleges or equivalent. B2. Masters degree or Higher Research Degree. B3. Hold, or working towards, a certificate in Academic Practice, or equivalent learning and teaching qualification. B4. Working knowledge of Public Dental Service patient acceptance criteria, patient flow, processes and interaction with other services. Skills Essential: C1. Ability to deliver research-informed teaching. C2. Excellent administrative and organisational skills. C3. Good IT skills including the ability to work in an online environment. C4. Excellent communication skills, both written and oral. C5. Excellent presentation skills. C6. Willingness and ability to work as part of a team. Experience Essential: E1. Minimum of 5 years post qualification with significant clinical experience in Adult Dentistry. E2. Experience of delivering undergraduate clinical teaching in Adult Dental Care settings. Desirable: F1. Experience in coordination of an undergraduate Dental Outreach programme. F2 .Experience in academic assessment of dental students. F3. Experience of mentoring students and/or staff. Job Features Staffing Glasgow Dental Hospital and School is the major dental referral and teaching centre in the West of Scotland and as such has a large number of NHS and University clinical staff of all grades. Administrative support within the Dental School reflects the course structure/curriculum with most Professional Services staff working within year teams whilst retaining some support for specialties. Academic staff members are responsible to: Professor A. Bell, Head of the Dental School for academic matters. The post holder will be mentored in this role and supported towards achieving a learning and teaching qualification, if not already in possession of such a qualification. Course structure The BDS programme is structured with each year being the unit of assessment and clinical specialties providing teaching within each year as appropriate. The dental course transitioned to the GDC’s Safe Practitioner Framework in 2025. Clinical Facilities Adult Outreach Dentistry is predominantly delivered within NHS GGC at the RAH (Paisley) and The Vale Centre for Health and Care (Vale of Leven). Glasgow Dental Hospital & School (Sauchiehall street, Glasgow) is the main location for delivery of Undergraduate Dental training. In the Final year of the BDS program, students spend half of their clinical time in an outreach setting. NHS education for Scotland provide access to 7 outreach centres aligned with the University of Glasgow dental school, across 6 health boards. Dimensions Involvement in the co-ordination and organisation of undergraduate teaching and assessment. Responsible for the teaching and assessment of undergraduate students. Mentoring staff and students, as appropriate. Planning and Organising Advance planning - ensuring adequate supply of suitable patients for treatment in an Adult Outreach setting. Working with the University outreach team to facilitate student placements and local logistics. Advance planning of teaching and preparation of lectures, tutorial and other teaching materials in line with the University’s quality assurance procedures and satisfying appropriate deadlines / timescales. Self-generation of work through scholarly pursuits plus innovation in teaching and administration. Organisational skills to ensure effective time management and prioritisation as appropriate for teaching, scholarship and administrative duties. Decision Making Prioritise own work. Teaching course content and assessment. Responsible for organisation of resources as appropriate. Internal/External Relationships Internal: Head of Dental School, Director of Dental Education and Head of Clinical Section, for exchanging information relating to the learning and teaching requirements. University of Glasgow Outreach team and Final year coordination team. Academic colleagues with regard to all aspects of the post Undergraduate students for teaching and learning support purposes. Academic support services for appropriate advice and for exchanging information. Student support services, to exchange information, refer/support students. NHS Public Dental Service NHS Secondary Care. External Academics in other HEIs to share good teaching practice and foster collaboration in relation to educational scholarship. Problem Solving To give advice/problem solve on learning and teaching issues to staff and students as appropriate. To resolve issues of an academic and clinical academic nature. Further Information For further information regarding Glasgow and the University: http://www.gla.ac.uk For further information regarding the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences: http://www.gla.ac.uk/colleges/mvls/ For further information regarding the Dental School: http://www.gla.ac.uk/schools/dental/ DETAILS OF ARRANGEMENTS FOR APPLICANT TO VISIT THE DENTAL SCHOOL In the first instance please contact: Professor Aileen Bell Head of School Phone: 0141 211 9701 or 9741 E-mail: aileen.bell@glasgow.ac.uk Or Mr James Donn, Final Year and Outreach Coordinator Phone: 0141 211 9626 E-mail: James.donn@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be on the Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale, £66,404 - £103,475 per annum. This post is full time (35 Hours Per Week) and open ended. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. The University of Glasgow is committed to safeguarding children and protected adults by ensuring that individuals in regulated roles are appropriately vetted and monitored. The University of Glasgow’s PVG policy aligns with the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020 and reflects the legal requirements effective from 1 April 2025, including the mandatory PVG Scheme membership for regulated roles. The post for which you are applying is considered to be a regulated role under the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020. Under the Act, individuals working in a regulated role must be a PVG Scheme member before employment. If you are successful in your application, the offer of employment will be conditional subject to a satisfactory PVG Scheme record check. We will be in touch regarding requirements related to this. Further information is available at : https://www.mygov.scot/pvg-scheme As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. For any informal enquiries, please contact Professor Aileen Bell, Head of Dental School, Aileen.Bell@glasgow.ac.uk or Mr James Donn, Final Year and Outreach Co-ordinator, James.Donn@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 12th November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Posted 1 day ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/mbchb-administrative-officer-6063343": {
      "title": "MBChB Administrative Officer · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "MBChB Administrative Officer · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer MBChB Administrative Officer Back to jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Job details Posted 16 October 2026 Salary Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 209813 Expiry 02 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description We have an exciting opportunity for an MBChB Administrative Officer to join the Undergraduate Medical School within the School of Medicine, Dentistry & Nursing. This is a full-time (35 hours per week), open-ended position. You will provide high-quality administrative support to enable the efficient and effective management, delivery and development of the General Practice led teaching and assessment components of the MBChB programme. The School of Medicine, Dentistry & Nursing is renowned for pioneering teaching methods and superb facilities for undergraduate and postgraduate study. The School of Medicine, Dentistry & Nursing is part of the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences. Job Purpose To provide high-quality administrative support to enable the efficient and effective management, delivery and development of the General Practice led teaching and assessment components of the MBChB programme. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Responsible for working in collaboration with Academic Leads and Senior Professional Services Managers to manage the operational delivery of General Practice led teaching and assessment as a fully integrated part of the MBChB programme. 2. Take a leading role in the development of policies and practices to improve the effectiveness and efficiency of General Practice led teaching and assessment delivery, taking account of all stakeholder requirements and available resources. 3. Lead and develop a high performing team of General Practice administrators to provide and enhance the support experience for staff and students. 4. In liaison with the Additional Costs of Teaching (ACT) Officer and ACT Budget Holder, prepare and administer the GP ACT budget for undergraduate education to ensure that teaching activities are accurately costed and reimbursed, expenditure projections are provided for future planning, and accurate reports are provided to the ACT Officer. 5. Responsible for the recruitment and allocation of General Practice Teaching Practices as necessary – with particular emphasis on sourcing placements as required throughout all years of the MBChB programme. This includes support for training and ongoing communication. 6. Management of all aspects of General Practice Tutor recruitment in conjunction with People & Organisational Development and according to recruitment policies and procedures. 7. Management of the planning and delivery of General Practice tutor training events, with particular emphasis on the annual training conference. 8. Responsible for overseeing the recruitment and payment of General Practice interviewers for Admissions Interviews and examiners for the OSCE examinations, ensuring that all relevant People & Organisational Development recruitment processes are adhered to. 9. Responsible for the organisation and coordination of marking arrangements for the Year 3 Longitudinal Portfolio (LP) assessment and Year 4 Ethics formative assessment, together with the subsequent preparation and processing of student results. 10. Contribute to the review and planning of General Practice led teaching through the Education Management Group (EMG) to ensure that programmes are effectively costed, evaluated and delivered. 11. Oversight of the awarding of annual General Practice related prizes, in liaison with the Academic GP Lead and Undergraduate Medical School Manager. 12. Assist with the set-up and on-the-day administration of OSCE assessments both on and off campus. 13. Undertake any other reasonable duties that either the Manager or Deputy Managers of the Undergraduate Medical School may request. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associatedwith this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills through personal development and progression in a similar role(s) OR Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 8 (Scottish Vocational Qualification level 4, Higher National Diploma) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. Desirable B1 Understanding and experience of administration in a higher education environment. B2 Knowledge of the National Health and Primary Care services. B3 Knowledge of working with systems used within the University of Glasgow such as Agresso, CoreHR, Moodle, MyCampus and T4 web publishing. Skills Essential C1 Ability to demonstrate operational, strategic planning and decision making in a large organisation. C2 Excellent verbal communication and interpersonal skills. C3 Ability to demonstrate tact, diplomacy and assertiveness to build excellent relationships with students and colleagues at all levels. C4 Able to communicate effectively in writing to a high standard for the purposes of report writing, documentation, correspondence and developing online content. C5 Experience of working independently and resolving unforeseen issues and challenges. C6 Ability to prioritise own workload and that of others effectively with the confidence to take decisions when necessary. C7 Ability to always deliver high levels of accuracy, attention to detail and professionalism. C8 Proven ability to work to deadlines and multitask across several complex tasks and processes. C9 Good numeracy skills and an understanding of financial planning. C10 Experience with Microsoft Office packages. Experience Essential E1 Experience of leading or managing an administration team, setting team objectives, determining priorities and managing performance. E2 Experience of handling sensitive and confidential information. E3 Experience of excellent service delivery and building stakeholder relationships. Desirable F1 Experience of working within the Higher Education sector supporting learning and teaching activities. F2 Experience in process reviews and continuous improvement. F3 Experience of clerking committees and producing accurate minutes of meetings. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum. This post is full time (35 Hours Per Week) and open ended. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries should be directed to Suzanne McDowall, Suzanne.McDowall@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 2nd November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Posted about 2 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-assistant-6062974": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Assistant Research Assistant Back to jobs Research Assistant Job details Posted 16 October 2026 Salary Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210513 Expiry 05 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose You will contribute to a project (‘Scotland Beyond Net Zero: A living lab approach to decarbonizing the Scottish festival sector - a case study of reusable cups and serveware’) working with Matt Brennan and Graeme Hunt in close collaboration with Edinburgh Napier University, DF Concerts & Events, and A Greener Future.  The postholder will work across the project partnership to co-design and deliver research into the use of reusable cups and serveware at Scottish music festivals. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of research publications and research proposals as well as contribute to project coordination and planned publication activity, under the guidance of the project lead(s). Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with and under the guidance of the Principal/Co Investigator: 1. Plan and conduct a comparative assessment of existing reusable cups and serveware systems in Scottish festivals. 2. Plan and conduct a survey of UK/Europe festivals sector current best practice regarding reusable cups and serveware. 3. Plan and conduct interviews with Scottish festival organisers to assess barriers to the use of reusables. 4. Co-design, alongside project partners, the living-lab data collection and evaluation framework. 5. Document research output including analysis and interpretation of all data, maintaining records and databases, drafting technical/progress reports and papers as appropriate. 6. Develop and enhance your research profile and reputation and that of The University of Glasgow, including contributing to publications of international quality in high profile/quality refereed publications, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 7. Contribute to the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research. 8. Collaborate with colleagues and participate in team/ group/ meetings/ seminars/ workshops across the project team. 9. Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the project. 10. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the project lead. Knowledge,Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 SCQF Level 10 (Honours degree) in the creative industries, festival and event management, and/or environmental sustainability, or a relevant subject or a cognate discipline, or equivalent. A2 Theoretical and practical knowledge of desk-based and social research mixed methods including literature reviews, surveys, and interviews. A3 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of the wider subject area or subject specialism. Desirable: B1 An awarded (or recently submitted or near completion) PhD in a relevant field. B2 Knowledge of CO2 emissions calculations in the events sector. B3 Knowledge of conducting life cycle assessments. Skills Essential: C1 Skills in carrying out research methods including literature reviews, surveys, and interviews, and analysing and writing up resulting data. C2 Research creativity and cross-discipline collaborative ability. C3 Excellent communication skills (oral and written), including public presentations and ability to communicate complex data/concepts clearly and concisely. C4 Excellent interpersonal skills including team working and a collegiate approach. C5 Appropriate workload/time/project/budget/people management skills. C6 Office 365 and data analysis/interpretation skills. C7 Self-motivation, initiative and independent thought/working. C8 Problem solving skills including a flexible and pragmatic approach. C9 Proven ability to deliver quality outputs in a timely and efficient manner. Experience Essential: E1 Sufficient relevant research experience (or equivalent) appropriate to an early career researcher. E2 Experience of applied research involving external stakeholders, and producing reports for non-academic audiences. E3 Experience of research report writing. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor Matt Brennan, Matt.Brennan@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata. This post is part time (14 hours per week) and fixed term for up to 16 weeks. This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website, https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/pod/visasimmigration/ As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date : 5 November 2026 @23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Assistant Glasgow Posted about 6 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/senior-lecturer-in-media-policy-slash-media-management-r-and-t-6061796": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T)",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Back to jobs Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Job details Posted 15 October 2026 Salary Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210453 Expiry 12 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description The Cultural Industries subject area in the School of Culture & Creative Arts, College of Arts and Humanities seeks to appoint a Senior Lecturer (Research & Teaching) in Media Policy or Media Management. Applications are invited from suitably qualified candidates who can lead teaching, research and industry engagement in one or more of the following: national and/or international media policy; media management; media regulation and governance, digital media, social media, media research methods, or other relevant and cognate areas. You will be expected to undertake research of international excellence and contribute to knowledge exchange activities related to the subject, contribute to learning and teaching on agreed programmes and undertake administration and service activities in line with the School/College’s strategic objectives. Job Purpose To make a substantial and positive contribution to the strategic direction of the School/College through leading and contributing to research of international standard, high quality teaching at both undergraduate and especially postgraduate level, securing research funding, and providing academic leadership and management within the School/College and the developing subject area of Cultural Industries.  To actively engage in relationships with external stakeholders that advance knowledge exchange, public understanding and outreach. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Undertake a leading role in the development and implementation of individual and/or joint research projects of international standard, participating actively in the development of College/School/Subject research strategy. 2. Act as principal investigator or project leader on major research projects with a portfolio of research activity and publications of high quality and impact. 3. Take a lead in major funding bids and related activities, including Knowledge Exchange, which develop and sustain support for the subject area and recognised excellence for the University. 4. Undertake academic management and leadership roles and responsibilities to ensure high quality course/programme administration, setting and monitoring standards and lead on the development of policy and practice across research and teaching teams (beyond subject/school level). 5. Create and develop effective networks with students, staff across the University, and external stakeholders to oversee the embedding of key University priorities such as graduate attributes, professional requirements, work-related learning, transitions support, student mobility and quality enhancement within credit bearing and non-credit bearing activities. 6. Develop a strategic approach to embedding learning technologies across the Subject/School and College to support and enhance course/programme delivery, course organisation, feedback and assessment within campus based, blended and online provision. 7. Lead the development of inclusive and innovative approaches to teaching, learning and assessment that recognise the needs of a diverse student cohort, taking appropriate responsibility for managing associated cultural changes and staff support and development. 8. Regularly attend and participate in appropriate activities to effectively contribute to the subject specialism, through learned societies, professional bodies, broader review and editorial processes. Disseminate research findings locally, nationally and internationally to enhance learning within the discipline and to represent the University externally. 9. Through an established network of external stakeholders, increasingly influence public understanding and/or professional policy and practice aligned with the strategic priorities of the School/College/University. 10. Supervise individual student projects at both undergraduate and especially postgraduate level to ensure their effective development and assisting with difficulties e.g. learning support/problems. 11. Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 12. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School. 13. To contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan, World Changers Together. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/ Qualifications Essential: A1 Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) or equivalent in related discipline  with an extensive and established reputation in research and significant teaching experience within the discipline/subject area. A2 Recognised expertise in teaching and researching one or more topics related to the taught Postgraduate programme MSc Media Management – such as national and/or international media policy; media management; media governance and regulation; media economics; digital media industries and policies; social media policy and regulation; cultural policy ; media research methods. A3 A thorough understanding of, and track record in, contributing to broader academic leadership and management at School/Service/College level. A4 sustained engagement with current practice and developing knowledge. A4 Engagement with the current and future priorities of University/College/School strategies, aims and ambitions. Desirable: B1 Ability to teach topics related to complementary subject programmes MA Creative Arts and Industries and/or MSc Global Cultural Enterprise – such as global and international media and cultural industries and/or policy; diverse cultural economies; social and community cultural economies; third sector production. B2 Experience of teaching media research methods and methodologies. Skills Essential: C1 Ability to disseminate conceptual and complex ideas to a wide variety of audiences to promote understanding. C2 Ability to undertake academic leadership of groups/activities with substantial impact on finance and other resources. C3 Ability to forge new and effective relationships with colleagues, internally and externally, to  foster collaboration across the University and with colleagues in other institutions. C4 Ability to demonstrate independent thought in order to generate original research and secure  research income. Desirable: D1 Ability to communicate a range of material to diverse international student cohorts. D2 Experience of course or programme management and/or leadership at undergraduate or postgraduate level. Experience Essential: E1 Proven ability to plan and lead staff and efficiently deploy resources in support of major research and teaching activities. E2 Substantial track record of published research of international standard compatible with enhancing the School’s submission to the REF. E3 Established track record of successfully securing funding for research projects, as appropriate for the subject specialism. E4 Internationally recognised excellence and reputation in subject area and scholarship of teaching in the discipline. E5 Proven track record of developing and devising teaching programmes, techniques and methods. E6 Track record of student supervision and development/mentorship/coaching of early career academic colleagues in subject area/team. E7 Demonstrated commitment to open research, as appropriate to the discipline, through open data, open code, open educational resources and practices that support replication. E8 Proven commitment to supporting the career development of colleagues and to other forms of collegiality appropriate to the career stage. Desirable: F1 demonstrable experience in building and maintaining media industry and/or policy contacts that can support teaching and research. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum. This post is full time (35 Hours Per Week) and open ended. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal Enquiries should be directed to Professor Mark Banks (Head of Subject, Cultural Industries), Mark.Banks@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 12th November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Posted 1 day ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-assistant-6060562": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Assistant Research Assistant Back to jobs Research Assistant Job details Posted 14 October 2026 Salary Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata End date 11 November 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 207794 Expiry 11 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose You will contribute to a project called AUTOWyrm working with Dr Kevin Worrall. This project is associated with developing a new robotic platform. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of research publications and research proposals as well as help manage and direct this complex and challenging project as opportunities allow. Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with and under the guidance of the Principal/Co Investigator: 1. Plan and conduct assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the project deliverables and project/group/School/College research strategy. 2. Document research output including analysis and interpretation of all data, maintaining records and databases, drafting technical/progress reports and papers as appropriate. 3. Develop and enhance your research profile and reputation and that of The University of Glasgow/School/Research Group, including contributing to publications of international quality in high profile/quality refereed journals, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 4. Contribute to the presentation of work at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance our research profile. 5. Contribute to the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research. 6. Collaborate with colleagues and participate in team/ group/ meetings/ seminars/ workshops across the research Group/School/College/University and wider community. 7. Contribute to the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 8. Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including Budgets/Expenditure. 9. Carry out modest Teaching activities [e.g. demonstrating etc.] and associated admin as assigned by the Head of School and in consultation with Principal Investigator. 10. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 11. Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 12. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School/Director of Research Institute. 13. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan, World Changers Together. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge and Qualifications Essential: A1 SCQF Level 10 [Honours degree] in a relevant subject or a cognate discipline, or equivalent A2 Specialist theoretical and practical knowledge of design, construction, and extensive testing of mechatronic systems for environments that can be considered extreme (Space, extraterrestrial bodies, extreme regions of the Earth) A3 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of the wider subject area or subject specialism Desirable: B1 An awarded [or recently submitted or near completion] PhD in subject specialism or equivalent Skills Essential: C1 Development of mechatronics for environments that can be considered extreme (Space, extraterrestrial bodies, extreme regions of the Earth) C2 Research creativity and cross-discipline collaborative ability as appropriate C3 Excellent communication skills [oral and written], including public presentations and ability to communicate complex data/concepts clearly and concisely C4 Excellent interpersonal skills including team working and a collegiate approach C5 Appropriate workload/time/project/budget/people management skills C6 Extensive IT and data analysis/interpretation skills as appropriate C7 Self-motivation, initiative and independent thought/working C8 Problem solving skills including a flexible and pragmatic appropriate Experience Essential E1 Sufficient relevant research experience [or equivalent] appropriate to an early career researcher E2 Project or subject specific experience E3 Experience of scientific writing E4 Proven ability to deliver quality outputs in a timely and efficient manner E5 Evidence of an emerging track record of publications in a relevant field Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata. This post is part-time (17.5 hours per week), and has funding available for up to 12 months in the first instance. Appointment to this post may be subject to the successful completion of a screening test requested by the funding body following the interview process. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  11 November 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Assistant Glasgow Posted 2 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
