    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-assistant-6059468": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Assistant Research Assistant Back to jobs Research Assistant Job details Posted 13 October 2026 Salary GRADE 6, £33,951-£37,694 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 209613 Expiry 26 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose You will contribute to the project “ SCN1A Horizons: A natural history study of *SCN1A-*related epilepsy in the United Kingdom” led by Chief Investigator Professor Andreas Brunklaus. The aim of this natural history study is to gain an in-depth understanding of the impact of Dravet syndrome and other SCN1A -related epilepsies on the patient and family, to help inform future treatment, support and research. Study participants undergo detailed clinical and neuropsychology assessments. The post-holder will be responsible for carrying out the neuropsychology visits with participants and their caregivers every 6- or 12-months, dependent upon age of participant. The research assistant will be fully trained to carry out a range of neurodevelopmental assessments, under the supervision of a Consultant Paediatric Neuropsychologist. The successful candidate will carry out hospital, home and school visits within Glasgow and will also travel across the rest of Scotland and North England to complete neuropsychology study visits. The post-holder will benefit from joining an established and experienced UK-wide multi-disciplinary research study team and will be supervised by a Consultant Paediatric Neuropsychologist. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Plan and conduct assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the project deliverables and project/group/School/College research strategy. 2. Conduct cognitive assessments with children and young people with rare genetic epilepsies, many of whom will present with significant developmental delay. 3. Travel independently to clinical research sites across Scotland, as required. 4. Document research output including analysis and interpretation of all data, maintaining records and databases, drafting technical/progress reports and papers as appropriate. 5. Develop and enhance your research profile and reputation and that of The University of Glasgow/School/Research Group, including contributing to publications of international quality in high profile/quality refereed journals, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit and gathering indicators of esteem. 6. Contribute to the presentation of work at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance our research profile. 7. Contribute to the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research. 8. Collaborate with colleagues and participate in team/ group/ meetings/ seminars/ workshops across the research Group/School/College/University and wider community. 9. Contribute to the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 10. Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including Budgets/Expenditure. 11. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 12. Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 13. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School/Director of Research Institute. 14. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan, Inspiring People Changing The World. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 SCQF Level 10 [Honours degree] in a relevant subject or a cognate discipline, or equivalent A2 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of the wider subject area or subject specialism of child cognitive development. Desirable B1 An awarded [or recently submitted or near completion] PhD in subject specialism or equivalent Skills Essential C1 Willingness to travel to carry out research visits, including hospital, home and school settings. C2 Research creativity and cross-discipline collaborative ability as appropriate. C3 Excellent communication skills [oral and written], including public presentations and ability to communicate complex data/concepts clearly and concisely. C4 Excellent interpersonal skills including team working and a collegiate approach. C5 Appropriate workload/time/project/budget/people management skills. C6 Extensive IT and data analysis/interpretation skills including experience of research databases. C7 Self-motivation, initiative and independent thought/working. C8 Problem solving skills including a flexible and pragmatic approach. Experience Essential E1  Demonstratable experience and desire to work with children in a clinical or research capacity. E2 Sufficient relevant research experience [or equivalent] appropriate to an early career researcher. E3 Experience of scientific writing. E4 Proven ability to deliver quality outputs in a timely and efficient manner. E5 Evidence of an emerging track record of publications in a relevant field. Desirable F1 Experience of working with children with learning disabilities and/or complex neurodevelopmental needs. F2 Experience of carrying out neurodevelopmental assessment of children using validated tools. F3 Experience of completing validated questionnaires/interview measures with caregivers. F4 Experience of scoring psychological validated assessment tools. Informal enquiries should be directed to :  SCN1AHorizons@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum. This post is full time, and has funding for up to 12 months in the first instance. The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. Protection of Vulnerable Groups (PVG) The University of Glasgow is committed to safeguarding children and protected adults by ensuring that individuals in regulated roles are appropriately vetted and monitored. The University of Glasgow’s PVG policy aligns with the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020 and reflects the legal requirements effective from 1 April 2025, including the mandatory PVG Scheme membership for regulated roles. The post for which you are applying is considered to be a regulated role under the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020. Under the Act, individuals working in a regulated role must be a PVG Scheme member before employment. If you are successful in your application, the offer of employment will be conditional subject to a satisfactory PVG Scheme record check. We will be in touch regarding requirements related to this. Further information is available at : https://www.mygov.scot/pvg-scheme As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date : 26 October 2026 @23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Assistant Glasgow Posted 3 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/graduate-teaching-assistant-peer-support-6058872": {
      "title": "Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Graduate Teaching Assistant - Peer Support · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Graduate Teaching Assistant - Peer Support Graduate Teaching Assistant - Peer Support Back to jobs Graduate Teaching Assistant - Peer Support Job details Posted 13 October 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type GTA and Demonstrator Reference 209533 Expiry 03 Nov 2026 22:59 Job description Applications for this post will only be processed where you are a University of Glasgow student and have been given the vacancy number by your Local School Administrator. Job Purpose Within a clear and established teaching programme, to effectively and efficiently undertake a range of duties associated with the peer support of full time and part time students and associated administration as requested by the Programme Director in order to contribute to School of Health and Wellbeing objectives. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1.    To provide peer support to SHW MSc students working towards their dissertations. 2.    Participate in a range of course administration duties, within required timescales, including effective communication of information and timeous constructive feedback. 3.    Assist with the supervision of student projects, dissertations, and any practical work, advising on skills, methods and techniques to assist the transfer of knowledge, and respond appropriately to the diverse range of learner support/ needs. 4.    Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 5.    Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School. 6.    To contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan. Qualifications A1Honours degree (SCQF Level 10) relevant to the teaching area or have expertise in a relevant field, together with an understanding of the principles of teaching, learning and assessment. A2Registered for and working towards the achievement of a PhD (MPhil/PhD) or PGT A3 qualification in a relevant subject. Knowledge, Skills and Experience C1 Expert knowledge of the subject area. C2 Experience of delivering and supporting undergraduate or postgraduate students either in an online, distance learning or face to face environment. C3 Excellent communication and presentation skills. C4 Ability to use IT and relevant software packages to support teaching and learning. C5 Proven ability to work independently and as part of a team. C6 Ability to work to deadlines. C7 Commitment to the University’s published values and professional behaviours. Apply Now Save this job Graduate Teaching Assistant - Peer Support Glasgow Posted 3 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/demonstrator-school-of-infection-and-immunity-6058809": {
      "title": "Demonstrator",
      "description": "Demonstrator - School of Infection & Immunity · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Demonstrator - School of Infection & Immunity Demonstrator - School of Infection & Immunity Back to jobs Demonstrator - School of Infection & Immunity Job details Posted 13 October 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type GTA and Demonstrator Reference 209893 Expiry 27 Oct 2026 22:59 Job description Applications for this post will only be processed where you are a University of Glasgow student and have been given the vacancy number by your Local School Administrator. Job Purpose To assist in classes/sessions in Microbiology and with the demonstration of simple laboratory methods for Postgraduate Taught/Masters students. To support teaching through the demonstration of exercises in line with agreed learning outcomes of the course. To offer assistance, as appropriate, including the provision of detailed explanations of how the activity relates to the wider area of study. To effectively and efficiently undertake a range of duties associated with the teaching of full time students from a range of backgrounds and associated administration as requested by the Head of School/Subject in order to contribute to the College/School/Subject Area objectives. Main Duties and Responsibilities To demonstrate in labs in the subject specialism of Microbiology and Molecular Biology techniques, and to answer questions related to those demonstrations, including the provision of detailed explanations of how the activity relates to the wider area of study. To assist/give guidance, where required, to students who are carrying out practical exercises, calculations and recording and analysis of datasets. To create a welcoming and respectful environment for students with a range of backgrounds and prior experiences, to enhance their learning, and to help them settle into Glasgow and the University. To be aware of any health and safety implications, and take action to maintain a safe environment, raising any concerns with the course organiser. To provide short talks and demonstrations at the start of each lab, supported by an illustrated whiteboard, to brief a group of max. 16 students on the background and work to be carried out in that lab, and to provide a short debrief at the end of each lab to discuss results and to provide general feedback on lab technique. Where required, to assist in marking student tests set by the course organiser using defined marking criteria where marking requires little or no interpretation. Where required, to provide factual feedback to students and the course organiser. Where required, to ensure that equipment, exercise or process is functional and/or material for demonstrations, exercises etc is prepared before the start of a session. Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School. To contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan, World Changers Together Please note that as part of your application you must address and demonstrate how you meet EACH of the essential / desirable criteria **.** If you do not address each criterion in the format explained above you will not be shortlisted for interview. Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1. Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 10 (Honours Degree) in subject specialism or equivalent. A2. Expert knowledge of relevant subject/class/session. A3. Awareness of learning outcomes of course. A4. Awareness of health and safety risks in microbiology labs. Skills Essential C1. Excellent presentation skills. C2. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Experience Essential E1. Previous experience of demonstrating to students. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum pro rata. This post is offered on a fractional basis and is fixed term. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Demonstrator - School of Infection & Immunity Glasgow Posted 3 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/retail-operations-team-leader-6058742": {
      "title": "Retail Operations Team Leader · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Retail Operations Team Leader · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Retail Operations Team Leader Retail Operations Team Leader Back to jobs Retail Operations Team Leader Job details Posted 13 October 2026 Salary £14.84 per hour Location Glasgow Reference 208294 Expiry 27 Oct 2026 22:45 Job description Please note this vacancy is only open to current contracted G.U. Heritage Retail Ltd and University of Glasgow employees. Role Purpose Engaging your team inclusively through clear and concise communications. Always making sure that your store is setup to deliver a great experience for our colleagues, members and customers. Using our Co-op behaviours to deliver our legal and operational standards every day, and in the absence of your Store Manager, you’ll take responsibility for managing the team and the store. Main Duties and Responsibilities Ensure store is safe, legal and operational *         Completing the daily safe and legal checks *         Carrying out stock and inventory routines as per plans *         Consistently maintaining high book stock accuracy standards to ensure our customers always have a quick and easy shopping experience *         Taking ownership for delivering promotional stock change *         Diligently carrying out all operational processes, routines and maintaining relevant controls *         Support your manager with people processes, for example, Employee Relations, notetaking, investigations and absence management reviews *         Continuously speaking up and suggest improvements to the success measures of your store Friendly & thoughtful service *         Championing Co-op by ensuring our members and customers are put first in all you do *         Coaching our service promises, to help colleagues put our customers first *         Going the extra mile by using your best judgement to deliver a friendly, welcoming, easier, helpful and thoughtful service everyday which meets customers’ needs and saves them time *         Ensuring the store is customer ready at all times during the day *         Always being on hand to help your colleagues and customers with queries *         In the absence of the store manager, take ownership for managing the store and handle potential unexpected situations e.g. out of hours call outs *         Ensuring best practice and procedures are adopted by colleagues to protect our customers and our brand reputation Cooperating for a fairer world *         Championing Co-op by seeking opportunities from colleagues, customers or members to cooperate for a fairer world *         Forging relationships and getting involved with your local community *         Collaborating with your Member Pioneer on the development and where relevant the delivery of our Co-op Community Plan Embrace change *         Enthusiastically leading our change activities and passionately engaging your team to seamlessly deliver change and embed ways of working *         Speaking up and encouraging the team to provide open and honest feedback on the effectiveness of change activities Engaging with everyone to succeed together *         Enabling an inclusive culture so everyone can speak up and their views are considered making colleagues feel respected and valued *         Forging strong relationships with colleagues in your store *         Adapting your communication style to meet the needs of the colleague or situation to reach a desired outcome i.e. coaching, challenging, engaging *         Building your teams capabilities by regular training *         Developing your team through regular and structured performance conversations to stretch their potential *         Building excitement and purpose in the team to understand how to get involved in their local community activity Online Order Fulfilment & Delivery Services Manage and monitor all online orders received through third-party delivery platforms such as Uber Eats and Deliveroo Ensure online orders are picked, packed and dispatched accurately and within agreed service times Maintain high standards of product quality, presentation and availability for online customers. Resolve customer and delivery partner queries relating to online orders professionally and efficiently Monitor delivery platform performance, including order acceptance rates, preparation times and customer feedback Liaise with delivery drivers and store colleagues to ensure smooth order fulfilment and minimise delays Support the management of stock availability to reduce substitutions and unavailable items on delivery platforms Ensure all online order procedures comply with food safety, age-restricted sales and company policies Identify opportunities to improve online sales performance, customer satisfaction and operational efficiency Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Understand how to engage and lead others to manage improved performance. A2 Understanding of online delivery platform operations, (including Uber Eats, Deliveroo and other click-and-delivery services.) A3 Knowledge of order fulfilment processes, service standards and customer expectations within an online retail environment. A4 Knowledge of your key partnerships with customer and community stakeholders. A5 Knowledge of legal compliance and store standards i.e. cash management, stock control. Skills Essential: C1 Coaching others to develop ways of working. C2 Effective and engaging communicator with an ability to use various methods. C3 Critical thinking and decision making to be able to problem solve in the moment, and for the future. C4 Ability to deal with multiple activities at the same time and prioritising where necessary. C5 Ability to manage multiple online and in-store priorities simultaneously while maintaining service standards. C6 Strong attention to detail to ensure order accuracy and minimise customer complaints. C7 Ability to proactively identify and resolve issues affecting online order fulfilment and delivery performance. C8 Competent in using digital ordering systems, handheld devices and delivery platform software. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of working in a fast-paced customer service environment. E2 Experience of managing and developing a team. E3 Experience of holding keys for the store. E4 Experience of working within a team to deliver success. E5 Experience of supporting the needs of colleagues and customers throughout the trading day. E6 Experience managing, processing and fulfilling online customer orders (through Uber Eats, Deliveroo or similar delivery platforms). E7 Experience working to online service metrics, including order preparation times, accuracy targets and customer satisfaction measures. E8 Experience handling customer queries and resolving issues relating to online orders and delivery services. Terms & Conditions Salary will be £14.84 per hour. This Team Leader role is full time (35 hours p/w) and open ended (permanent). Flexibility will be required as shift pattern will be varied to cover operational hours between 06.00 and 22.00, 5 over 7 days. Closing date: 27 October 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. GU Heritage Retail Ltd is a subsidiary company wholly owned by the University of Glasgow and provides retail services alongside branded and bespoke products to customers from across the University and beyond. The GUHRL team runs the University Shop, the Hunterian Shop, the University's Online Shop, and supplies University departments with branded merchandise. Apply Now Save this job Retail Operations Team Leader Glasgow Posted 3 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs MBChB Administrative Officer Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata Senior Lecturer in Media Policy/Media Management (R&T) Glasgow Grade 9: £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical University Lecturer in Adult Outreach Dentistry Glasgow Speciality Doctor and Dentist Pay Scale: £66,404 - £103,475 per annum Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/clinical-research-fellow-6065004": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Clinical Research Fellow · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Clinical Research Fellow Clinical Research Fellow Back to jobs Featured Clinical Research Fellow Job details Posted 20 October 2026 Salary Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Clinical Reference 210373 Expiry 17 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description The School of Cardiovascular & Metabolic Health is seeking to appoint two Clinical Research Fellows. You will contribute to therapy development research for angina and no obstructive coronary arteries (ANOCA) in women, led by Professor Colin Berry and funded by Wellcome Leap’s Visible Programme. This is a transformative international research initiative aiming to improve the diagnosis and treatment of women living with ANOCA. Women are disproportionately affected by coronary microvascular dysfunction and coronary vasospasm, yet these conditions remain under-recognised, resulting in delayed diagnosis, treatment uncertainty, and significant health inequalities. This pioneering, patient-centred programme is co-designed with women who have lived experience of ANOCA and led by internationally recognised clinical researchers and trialists. The programme brings together three closely linked studies: ANOCA Diagnosis, which seeks to improve the identification and classification of disease mechanisms, ANOCA Therapy, an innovative clinical trials platform designed to evaluate and accelerate access to effective treatments, and ANOCA Exercise, which will evaluate resistance exercise as a lifestyle therapy option. This is a unique opportunity to be part of a once-in-a-generation effort to generate the evidence needed to transform clinical care, improve therapy options for women, and address longstanding sex-based disparities in cardiovascular health. Job Purpose This is a fixed term (3 years) Clinical Research Fellowship tenable in the School of Cardiovascular and Metabolic Health. It is expected that the Fellow will register for a higher degree and that the work will provide material sufficient to submit an MD or PhD. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Responsible for supporting recruitment to clinical studies and data collection. 2. Supporting project management and study coordination in specified projects. 3. Analysis of data for efficacy assessments e.g. the MRI scans, the ECGs, and coronary angiograms, as directed and supervised by the principal investigators. 4. Enabling source data collection across sites. 5. Work closely with the Chief Investigator to support communication to collaborators, stakeholders, including patients and their carers/relatives. 6. Supporting trial management and compliance with Good Clinical Practice. 7. Contribute to outputs of the trial including abstracts and scientific papers. The research activity and data collected will support a Higher Degree. 8. Responsible for presenting at dissemination events including collaboration meetings and scientific symposia related to the School of Cardiovascular and Metabolic Health. 9. Support collaboration with other Wellcome Leap projects associated with this programme. 10. Contribute to clinical cardiology research in the BHF Glasgow Cardiovascular Research Centre, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, and the Golden Jubilee National Hospital under the supervision of Professor Berry, as appropriate. 11. You will be expected to register for a higher degree (e.g. MD or PhD) and the work is expected to contribute to the basis for submission of a higher degree. Training in relevant methodologies (e.g. Good Clinical Practice, MRI, ECG, quantitative coronary analyses) will be undertaken and certification maintained throughout the study. 12. A contribution to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching activities within the School may be required but will not form a major component of the post. 13. You will support all necessary administrative activities associated with Professor Berry’s Projects. Any material generated can be presented at local, national or international meetings. 14. As part of this project, this position will support clinical activities at the Clinical Research Facility at the Queen Elizabeth University Hospital in the speciality of Cardiology and also at the outpatient clinic at Gartnavel General Hospital. 15. You will support project coordination at the external sites participating in this project. 16. You will be expected to submit grant application(s) as appropriate to the needs of the project. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1. MBChB or equivalent. A2. MRCP (UK) or equivalent. A3. Completed general professional training in general medicine. A4. Full registration with General Medical Council & Licence to Practise. Desirable: B1. BSc or equivalent intercalated degree in medicine or related subject. Skills Essential: C1. Self-motivation and the ability to work independently. C2. Good organisational and IT skills. C3. Ability to manage research projects. C4. Ability to work towards research objectives of the project. C5. Excellent communication skills (patients, research staff, line manager). C6. Evidence of distinction in career to date e.g. prizes, awards, bursaries. C7. Strong interpersonal skills, ability to get on well with colleagues but also strong ambitions in the field. Experience Essential E1. Clinical experience in cardiology (e.g. at least ST3 or Clinical Fellow in Cardiology). E2. Experience in cardiac magnetic resonance imaging. Desirable: F1. Evidence of publication or experience in writing. F2. Previous involvement in clinical studies including courses relevant to clinical studies. F3. Experience with MRI image analysis software. F4. Experience in invasive cardiology. F5. Capacity for originality and innovation. Job Features The Fellow will be joining a clinical research team within the School and perform 100% of their duties under the auspices of the University of Glasgow. It is envisaged that the 3 year post would attract existing trainees in Cardiology wishing experience in clinical research. The job will involve clinical research activities in the Clinical Research and Imaging facilities in the Queen Elizabeth University Hospital, the outpatient clinic at Gartnavel Hospital, the NHS Golden Jubilee hospital, the sponsor team and coordination with site staff and the Robertson Centre for Biostatistics. The Graduate School of the School of Medicine supports all aspects of postgraduate training and development. It is expected that the Fellow will register for a higher degree and that the work will provide material sufficient to submit an MD or PhD. Postgraduate students undertaking higher degrees are required to participate in the Research Training Programme in generic and transferable skills coordinated by the Graduate School. Full details of the programme are available at http://www.gla.ac.uk/colleges/mvls/graduateschool/. In addition to a Supervisor and Co-supervisor, all postgraduate students will have an academic advisor appointed to provide mentoring. Progress will be reviewed. Terms and Conditions Salary will be on the Clinical Academic Scale £52,229 - £77,389 per annum. The post will carry an additional entitlement depending on on-call responsibilities. This banding payment is paid under a separate arrangement with the NHS for which the University acts as paymaster only. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and fixed term for up to 3 years. An honorary StR Contract will be required and applied for on appointment to NHS Greater Glasgow and Clyde and to the NHS Golden Jubilee. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. The University of Glasgow is committed to safeguarding children and protected adults by ensuring that individuals in regulated roles are appropriately vetted and monitored. The University of Glasgow’s PVG policy aligns with the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020 and reflects the legal requirements effective from 1 April 2025, including the mandatory PVG Scheme membership for regulated roles. The post for which you are applying is considered to be a regulated role under the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020. Under the Act, individuals working in a regulated role must be a PVG Scheme member before employment. If you are successful in your application, the offer of employment will be conditional subject to a satisfactory PVG Scheme record check. We will be in touch regarding requirements related to this. Further information is available at: https://www.mygov.scot/pvg-scheme As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be directed to Professor Colin Berry, Colin.Berry@glasgow.ac.uk. Closing Date: 17th November 2026 at 23:45 Apply Now Save this job Clinical Research Fellow Glasgow Posted 7 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
