    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/deputy-director-of-procurement-6068350": {
      "title": "Deputy Director of Procurement",
      "description": "Deputy Director of Procurement · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Deputy Director of Procurement Deputy Director of Procurement Back to jobs Deputy Director of Procurement Job details Posted 23 October 2026 Salary Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 210913 Expiry 09 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Please note this vacancy is open to University of Glasgow employees only Job Purpose Responsible along with the Director of Procurement, for the strategic development and Leadership of the Procurement team and aligning it operationally with the overall University strategy and external driver such as legislation and Scottish Executive initiatives. Ensure that Procurement Policies, Strategies and Procedures are maintained and implemented across the University and support best practice activity and robust governance, maximising value for money at all times. Support and deliver Sustainable Procurement practices that achieves value for money on all external spend. Provide effective leadership, management and support to all Category Managers and Procurement team members providing leadership, guidance and advice as required. Main duties and responsibilities 1. To develop, implement and maintain the University's procurement strategy and supporting policy and procedural frameworks 2. Leading and managing a professionally qualified Procurement team, by establishing and embedding quality and performance standards and measures and developing and improving the capability of individuals in order to achieve operational and strategic goals 3. Provide leadership and management to the team, overseeing all projects and providing strategic advice to senior executives and the team as necessary with an emphasis on keeping in line with strategy and value for money. 4. Provide specialist advice and guidance where necessary on complex projects covering procedural, legal and technical issues 5. Personally, manage a tender portfolio including the more complex category areas, from conception to contract preparation, debrief and award 6. Lead the process for all Capital tenders within Estates and Buildings working as a strategic partner with the E&B leadership teamwork with Senior Finance Colleagues and support the Finance Director to ensure the financing profile and contractual arrangements provide best value for money for the University during the life span of the Campus Development Strategy 7. Applying high level expertise and broad knowledge to provide procurement and contractual guidance and advice to all staff across the University on a wide range of issues directly and indirectly related to procurement and business procedures. This advice will often include legal advice and will require an interpretation of legal and internal rules. 8. Source appropriate external advice when necessary to assist in managing risk for the University in relation to more complex contracts 9. Evaluate existing processes to ensure robust and legally compliant systems are in place to promote best value procurement in a developed environment. 10. Propose and implement changes required to current policies such as Procurement Policy, C&SRM Policy, Guidance for less than and above £25k over 48 months, Travel, Purchasing Cards, and Procurement procedures (as part of Financial Procedures). 11. Devise and provide training and advice to staff and monitor departmental activity (made visible by the Purchase to Pay process) with a view to improving compliance and developing new opportunities 12. Research and prepare and take responsibility for strategic documents as required; for example, Procurement Policy, Procurement Strategic Plan, annual report, response to audit reports and creation of Procurement team performance monthly reports, ad-hoc reports and policies as required. 13. Create, promote and implement the University compliance to the Procurement rules and regulations against all spend (dashboard report) monthly. 14. Create improvement plans for non-compliant spend 15. Continuously improve Procurement practice across the University to enable achievement of Chartered Institute of Procurement and Supply (CIPS) Corporate Certification for the University of Glasgow, with ongoing monitoring and audit compliance 16. Lead initiatives to deliver procedural and efficiency improvements across the University and promote best practice. Projects will include: - Maximise the value benefit of E Procurement, working collaboratively with colleagues in systems and across the University to ensure the project is managed effectively and suppliers are adopted, and end users are made aware of how to access the contracts vie e procurement. - Create, implement and refresh Category Strategies for key budget holders. - Enhance and improve the centralised documentation control for all procurements, following the Scottish Government procurement journey. - Lead and deliver internal training programme for all Purchasing Officers. - Create and manage internal audits for all areas across the University for spend less than £25k. - Effective management of the Purchase to Pay (P2P) project to maximise value for money and process improvements required for all aspects of the Procurement function working in collaboration with the Finance department. 17. Represent and promote the university externally through membership of various collaborative, inter university and external groups 18. Acting on behalf of the University provide strategic input to groups such as Scottish Universities Heads of Procurement Group, Research Equipment Affinity Group (funded by HEFCE) and Procurement Strategy Group (Centre of Collaborative Expertise). Also respond to occasional invites to assist on external groups, eg Race Relations Board, Scottish Executive 19. Measure and monitor through Contract & Supplier Relationship Management (C&SRM) key strategic Suppliers to ensure value assurance is being achieved for the University 20. Ensure robust Terms and Conditions across Goods and Services and other forms of Contractual / Framework Agreements for Estates & Buildings Agreements are in place and refreshed appropriately to mitigate risk to the University 21. Create, promote and monitor the Sustainable Procurement Policy by Measure and monitor Supplier / Contractor CIPS Sustainability Indexation ratings. Review and address performance with the of creation action plans as required and promote and influence key strategic Suppliers to participate in the university sustainability review via our third-party provider EcoVadis 22. Maintain, or enhance the University status for the Chartered Institute of Procurement and Supply (CIPS) awards such as the CIPS Procurement Excellence award. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the necessary strategic competencies required to undertake the duties associated with the level of post having acquired the necessary professional knowledge and leadership experience in a similar or number of different specialist roles. A2 Degree qualified. A3 Highly developed knowledge of Public Sector Procurement Rules and Regulations. A4 Highly developed contract law and general legal knowledge. A5 Knowledge of modern procurement techniques, e.g. E Tendering. A6 Knowledge and understanding of Circular Procurement. A7 Knowledge and understanding of Sustainable Procurement. Desirable: B1 Relevant second degree. B2 Deep understanding of academic culture and context. B3 Financial control issues, processing techniques and systems. B4 Membership of Chartered Institute of Purchasing and Supply (MCIPS). Skills Essential: C1 High level of IT skill required. C2 Ability to interact robustly when required and yet diplomatically with wide range of people from internal staff at all levels to external suppliers, inter University colleagues, funding bodies etc. C3 Staff management and leadership. C4 Ability to develop and maintain policies and procedures and systems to measure activity and performance. C5 Leadership and motivational skills in formal and informal situations. C6 Excellent negotiation and diplomacy skills. C7 Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills. Desirable: D1 Wider aspects of higher education policies and issues (e.g. student interest in some suppliers, income streams etc.). Experience Essential: E1 Significant experience with exposure to a number of different categories and preferably sectors. E2 Engagement with high level policy making. E3 Experience of engaging and providing strategic advice to senior executives and guiding a professional team. E4 Experience of managing and supporting a team. Desirable: F1 Cross public sector procurement and/or private sector experience in policy and/or finance and/or procurement. F2 Committee representation. F3 Management of a team of professional staff. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum. This post is full time (Monday - Friday 35 hours per week) and open ended. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. This post will close at 23.45 on Monday 9th November 2026 Apply Now Save this job Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Posted 4 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/clinical-lecturer-in-child-health-6068068": {
      "title": "Clinical Lecturer in Child Health",
      "description": "Clinical Lecturer in Child Health · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Back to jobs Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Job details Posted 23 October 2026 Salary Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Clinical Reference 211276 Expiry 09 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description We have an exciting opportunity for a Clinical Lecturer in Child Health   in the School of Medicine, Dentistry & Nursing. This post is fixed term (up to 6 years or up until six months after obtaining CCT, whichever is soonest). You will hold a MRCPCH or equivalent and will have completed general professional training in paediatrics. It is expected that you will already hold a higher research degree or have embarked on a research programme that will lead to a MD or a PhD. The successful candidate will be appointed at the appropriate level of Specialty Trainee and will enter the West of Scotland Higher Training Programme in Paediatrics. You must already have a national training number at the time of taking up the post. The post will be based within the School of Medicine in the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences (MVLS) of the University of Glasgow.  Whilst clinical training will be a major focus of the Lectureship in order to ensure you can successfully complete your certificate of completion of training (CCT), this post will also provide an exceptional opportunity for combined academic training. Clinical training will focus on a subspeciality in paediatrics, and it is anticipated that clinical training will take place within the main University hospital sites – predominantly at the Royal Hospital for Children, Glasgow. Research training will be undertaken in the world class academic environment of the University of Glasgow with excellent opportunities within the School of Medicine, Dentistry & Nursing and any applicants with research interests that are aligned with College and School strategy are encouraged to apply. Job Purpose The academic aim of the lectureship would be to allow research development to support intermediate fellowship applications by the end of the lectureship and eventual growth to an independent academic career. Main Duties and Responsibilities Research The appointee will have an established interest in research with a record of achievement in an area relevant to existing interests in the School of Medicine within the College of MVLS. The appointee will be expected to: -        Undertake a programme of research under supervision of senior investigators in the School of Medicine, in a subject area in basic and/or translational and/or clinical science -        Plan, design and implement projects with collaborators as appropriate; and to contribute to and submit grant applications and publications of impactful papers. The appointee will be located within Child Health at the Royal Hospital for Children, School of Medicine. The appointee will also be expected to build links with other schools and institutes within the College. Academic support will be available during all annual research and training assessments to ensure that there is adequate balance between academic and clinical training. In addition to annual reviews of clinical progress, there will be reviews of academic progress within the Institute. Teaching The anticipation is that protected non-clinical time will be predominantly used for research. This post is not suitable for trainees wishing to develop a predominantly teaching or educational portfolio. However, it is expected that the Lecturer will contribute to delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate teaching activities. These might include; -        Teaching of clinical skills and other vocational training activities, including seminars and tutorials. -        Teaching or supervision within Postgraduate taught or research courses within the School of Medicine Administrative Duties The Lecturer will contribute to administrative duties related to research and general running of the School of Medicine or research theme as directed by their supervisor. Clinical Duties Clinical duties will be arranged following discussion with the relevant Training Programme Director in Paediatrics. The purpose of a Clinical Lectureship is to ensure that candidates acquire a CCT, and appropriate clinical training opportunities will be arranged to ensure that this can occur within the duration of the Lectureship. Responsibilities, including on call duties and specialty training will be arranged as necessary depending on the appointee's current training and needs in conjunction with the Training Program Director and local clinical and educational supervisors. Clinical academic training will be the focus of the post with an 80:20 split between clinical and academic activity. The allocation of research and clinical time is flexible and various patterns of working are possible to suit differing research and clinical requirements. Applications will be welcome from trainees who are working less than full time, and the job plan will be adapted to suit such trainees. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1. MB ChB or equivalent and full registration with General Medical Council. (for non-UK candidates, GMC registration would be required before taking up the post). A2. MRCPCH or equivalent. A3. Completed general professional training in paediatrics. A4. Applicants must already have a national training number in paediatrics at the time of taking up the post. Desirable: B1. BMedSci, BSc or equivalent intercalated degree in medicine or related subject. B2. Enrolled on a MD/PHD programme of research. B3. Submitted/awarded a MD or PhD award. Experience/Training Essential: C1. Clinical experience in paediatrics following completion of FY2. C2. Clinical or relevant research experience. Desirable: D1. Previous evidence of oral and poster presentations. D2. Evidence of publication or experience in writing. Special Aptitudes and Ability Essential: E1. Self-motivation and the ability to work independently. E2. Good organisational and IT skills. E3. Ability to manage research projects E4. Ability to work towards the Institute, College and University objectives. E5. Excellent communication skills with all stakeholders in research. Desirable: F1. Evidence of distinction in career to date e.g. prizes, awards, bursaries. Interpersonal Skills Essential: G1. Strong interpersonal skills, ability to get on well with colleagues but also strong ambitions in the field. Desirable: H1. Capacity for originality and innovation. H2. Experience of teaching and/or mentoring junior colleagues and undergraduates. JOB FEATURES INFORMATION REGARDING SCHOOL AND UNIVERSITY For further information regarding Glasgow and the University: http://www.gla.ac.uk/visitors/ For further information regarding the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences: http://www.gla.ac.uk/colleges/mvls/ For further information regarding the School of Medicine, Dentistry and Nursing: https://www.gla.ac.uk/schools/medicine/ Details of the activities of the mental health and wellbeing group can be found on our webpages: http://www.gla.ac.uk/schools/healthwellbeing/research/mentalhealth/ The Graduate School of the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences supports all aspects of postgraduate training and development. Its Clinical Academic Training Advisory Committee (CATAC) (Chair, Professor Christian Delles) arranges mentorship and support for Clinical Lecturers and Clinical Academic Trainees within the College.  The committee identifies and appoints an academic mentor who will ensure that the trainee’s clinical experience is maintained without being overly intrusive on his/her research development; the mentor will represent the trainee’s interests in any interaction with the Postgraduate Dean and will ensure that he/she receives appropriate career advice and guidance. The Committee receives annual reports on progress of trainees and will take action where there is evidence of difficulty. Additionally, the Committee arranges annual sessions for clinical trainees that provide access to an informal network of support and more formal sessions on aspects of clinical research and clinical academic training. All Clinical Lecturers and Clinical Academic Trainees shall have access to the extensive Research Training Programme in generic and transferable skills coordinated by the Graduate School. TEACHING AND LEARNING COURSES It is recommended that the appointee will attend appropriate courses on teaching and learning provided by the Teaching and Learning Service. PERFORMANCE AND DEVELOPMENT REVIEW The University is committed to undertaking Performance and Development Review (P&DR) for all of its employees with the goal of maximising individual and organisational performance while supporting personal development. Clinical academic staff who hold honorary Specialty Training contracts with the NHS have been opted out of the standard University P&DR framework, on the understanding that the appointee will undergo an Annual Review of Clinical Progress which will be informed by an Annual Academic Progress Report. Terms and Conditions Salary will be on the Clinical Academic Scale £52,229 - £77,389 per annum. The post will carry an additional entitlement depending on on-call responsibilities. This banding payment is paid under a separate arrangement with the NHS for which the University acts as paymaster only. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and has funding for up to 6 years. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. The University of Glasgow is committed to safeguarding children and protected adults by ensuring that individuals in regulated roles are appropriately vetted and monitored. The University of Glasgow’s PVG policy aligns with the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020 and reflects the legal requirements effective from 1 April 2025, including the mandatory PVG Scheme membership for regulated roles. The post for which you are applying is considered to be a regulated role under the Disclosure (Scotland) Act 2020. Under the Act, individuals working in a regulated role must be a PVG Scheme member before employment. If you are successful in your application, the offer of employment will be conditional subject to a satisfactory PVG Scheme record check. We will be in touch regarding requirements related to this. Further information is available at: https://www.mygov.scot/pvg-scheme As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. For informal enquiries, please contact: Professor Syed Faisal Ahmed, Faisal.Ahmed@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 9th November 2026  at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Posted 4 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-assistant-6067412": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Assistant Research Assistant Back to jobs Research Assistant Job details Posted 22 October 2026 Salary Grade 6:  £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata End date 05 November 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210995 Expiry 05 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose You will contribute to the GKEF-funded project Media Literacy in Practice: Evidence-Based Approaches to Countering Misinformation, led by Professor Anja Neundorf at the University of Glasgow and delivered in partnership with Shout Out UK and academic collaborators. The project translates experimental research comparing different modes of media literacy training into practical guidance for organisations seeking to strengthen citizens’ resilience to misinformation. Its principal outputs include a digital practitioner toolkit, a national implementation summit, post-summit webinars and implementation support, and evidence of organisational engagement and uptake. Working under the direction of the Principal Investigator and in collaboration with the wider project team, you will support the coordination and delivery of the project. You will contribute particularly to toolkit development, stakeholder mapping and engagement, organisation of the national summit and follow-up activities, preparation of accessible dissemination materials, and monitoring and documentation of project outcomes and impact. Attendance in London for the national implementation summit (scheduled in April or May 2027) is required. Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with and under the guidance of the Principal Investigator: 1.  Support the development of the National Media Literacy Practitioner Toolkit by reviewing relevant practitioner resources, coordinating contributions from the research team and Shout Out UK, and helping to translate research findings into clear and accessible guidance. 2.  Assist with the preparation of evidence summaries, practical implementation guidance and dissemination materials covering the effectiveness, cost-effectiveness, scalability, advantages and limitations of different media literacy delivery formats. 3.  Co-develop and maintain a stakeholder map covering organisations that deliver, commission, fund or regulate media literacy activities, including civil society organisations, local authorities, educators, libraries, policymakers, regulators and relevant funders. 4.  Support stakeholder recruitment and engagement for project activities, including identifying appropriate contacts, managing invitations and responses, maintaining accurate contact records, and communicating with participating organisations. 5.  Coordinate practical preparations for the hybrid National Media Literacy Implementation Summit in London, including participant communication, registration, event materials, speaker coordination and hybrid participation arrangements. 6.  Support the organisation and delivery of three post-summit webinars and related implementation and peer-learning activities, including scheduling, recruitment, participant communication and preparation of supporting materials. 7.  Assist with the development and administration of short stakeholder questionnaires and other monitoring tools, including programming surveys in Qualtrics, maintaining records and collating responses. 8.  Support follow-up engagement with participating organisations to document their use of the toolkit, intended or actual changes to practice, and barriers and facilitators to implementing evidence-based media literacy activities. 9.  Collate and summarise quantitative and qualitative information arising from stakeholder engagement, surveys, webinars and follow-up discussions, contributing to implementation case studies, learning summaries and reports on project outcomes and impact. 10.  Maintain clear and accurate project documentation, including stakeholder records, activity logs, meeting notes, timelines, task trackers and evidence required for funder reporting and potential future impact case studies. 11.  Work collaboratively with colleagues at the University of Glasgow, academic collaborators, Shout Out UK and participating stakeholder organisations, contributing to project meetings and communicating progress against agreed tasks and deadlines. 12.  Contribute to dissemination of the project’s findings and resources through practitioner briefings, presentations, online materials and other appropriate channels. 13.  Keep up to date with relevant research, policy developments and practitioner guidance in media literacy, misinformation and knowledge exchange. 14.  Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 15.  Undertake any other reasonable project-related duties as required by the Principal Investigator or Head of School. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge & Qualifications Essential A1 SCQF Level 10, normally an Honours degree, in politics, social science, communication, education or another relevant discipline, or equivalent professional experience. A2 Knowledge of research methods relevant to the social sciences. Desirable B1 A completed or ongoing postgraduate qualification in a relevant social science discipline. B2 Knowledge of research relating to misinformation, media literacy, political communication, civic education or democratic engagement. B3 Knowledge of the UK media literacy policy and practitioner landscape. B4 Understanding of knowledge exchange, research impact or stakeholder engagement. Skills Essential C1 Excellent written communication skills, including the ability to translate research findings and complex information into clear, concise and accessible materials for non-academic audiences. C2 Excellent oral communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to communicate professionally with academic colleagues, external partners and stakeholder organisations. C3 Strong organisational and project-management skills, including the ability to coordinate several activities, maintain accurate records and meet fixed deadlines. C4 Ability to conduct targeted desk research and synthesise information from academic, policy and practitioner sources. C5 Ability to work independently under appropriate supervision, exercise initiative and identify practical solutions to emerging problems. C6 Ability to work collaboratively within a multidisciplinary team and with external partner organisations. C7 Competence in standard digital and office applications, including document preparation, spreadsheets, online meeting platforms and shared project-management systems. C8 Ability to handle research and stakeholder information responsibly and in accordance with data-protection and research-ethics requirements. C9 Willingness and ability to travel to London to attend and support delivery of the national implementation summit. Desirable D1 Familiarity with Qualtrics or comparable online survey software. D2 Ability to collate, organise and summarise quantitative and qualitative information. D3 Familiarity with digital toolkits, interactive decision tools or online practitioner resources. Experience Essential E1 Experience of supporting or coordinating a research, knowledge-exchange, policy or stakeholder-engagement project. E2 Experience of conducting desk-based research and preparing written summaries or other project materials. E3 Experience of communicating and working effectively with colleagues or external stakeholders. E4 Proven ability to deliver high-quality work within agreed timescales. Desirable F1 Experience of organising workshops, webinars, conferences or hybrid events. F2 Experience of recruiting or communicating with research participants, practitioners or policy stakeholders. F3 Experience of administering surveys and managing research or monitoring data. F4 Experience of preparing practitioner-facing, policy-facing or public-facing research materials. F5 Experience of working collaboratively with civil society organisations, local authorities, educators, policymakers or similar external partners. Informal enquiries should be directed to Professor Anja Neundorf on Anja.Neundorf@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata. This post is part time (10.5 hours per week) and is fixed term term until 4 June 2027 in the first instance. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  5 November 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Assistant Glasgow Posted 4 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/technician-6067214": {
      "title": "Technician",
      "description": "Technician · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Technician Technician Back to jobs Technician Job details Posted 22 October 2026 Salary Grade 5: £28,031 - £31,236 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Technical And Specialist Reference 211074 Expiry 19 Nov 2026 22:45 Job description This post is based within the School of Infection and Immunity, working in the lab of Dr Abhinay Ramaprasad (Principal Investigator; www.parafugelab.com) whose main research focus is to study genes that control parasite replication within red blood cells, a critical process that enables malaria parasites to multiply and progress through the clinically significant blood-stage infection. The postholder will use state-of-the-art gene-editing technologies such as CRISPR-Cas9 and DiCre to inducibly knock out essential genes in the parasite. Job Purpose To provide in-depth technical expertise for creating inducible gene knockouts in human malaria parasites ( Plasmodium falciparum ) and provide advice on the set up and best operation of equipment and procedures to aid delivery objectives. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Provide in-depth technical support for generation of inducible gene knockout mutant parasite lines. This involves developing and implementing streamlined molecular cloning protocols to produce transfection-ready plasmids at scale. 2. Provide assistance and technical support relating to malaria culture work in a Containment Level 3 laboratory. This involves day-to-day maintenance of P. falciparum cultures and transfections to produce genetically modified parasite lines. 3. In liaison with PI to maintain the group’s laboratory equipment and diagnose faults and troubleshoot day-to-day problems relating to equipment, techniques, procedures, and protocols. 4. In liaison with the PI, advise, implement, and evaluate improvements to existing protocols relevant as required. 5. Collect, analyse and interpret data using standard procedures and ensure that accurate research records are kept, and databases are updated accordingly. 6. Plan and organise day-to-day work to ensure that the experimental goals are delivered within previously agreed timescales. 7. To supervise junior staff, where appropriate, and instruct, train and provide guidance to research staff and students in the techniques and operation of equipment and apparatus as directed by the supervisor, lab manager or PI. 8. Contribute to the well organised functioning of the laboratory and assist the PI/laboratory manager in maintaining the stock levels and purchasing consumables and supplies, within budget, and in consultation with other members of the group. 9. In consultation with the PI/laboratory manager, ensure the safe and efficient functioning of the laboratory area and facilities. This includes, but is not limited to, supporting the completion of risk assessments and COSHH assessments, ensuring that all Health and Safety paperwork is completed timeously and recorded accurately, and implementing and adhering to health and safety policies, as determined by the University and the Health and Safety Executive. 10. Collaborate with colleagues and participate in team meetings/discussions and departmental research group activities. 11. Keep up to date with advancements in technology and methodology, continually updating technical knowledge. 12. Be aware of and comply with Equality and Diversity policies of the University of Glasgow. The above duties are not exhaustive, and the post-holder will be required to undertake any other relevant duties as required by the PI/line manager which are commensurate with the grade of the post. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge, Qualifications Essential: A1. Scottish Credit and Qualifications Framework level 7 (Advanced Higher / HNC, SVQ 3), or equivalent in relevant science subject A2. Detailed technical knowledge of molecular biology and cell/tissue culture techniques. A3. Up to date knowledge of relevant laboratory Health and Safety legislation. Desirable: B1. Scottish Credit and Qualifications Framework level 9 (BSc Degree, SVQ Level 4) or equivalent in relevant scientific discipline. B2. Knowledge of Equality and Diversity policy. Skills Essential: C1. Skills in molecular biology. C2. Skills in cell and tissue culture and aseptic techniques. C3. IT, including familiarity with Microsoft Office programs and relevant databases. C4. Demonstrable analytical and problem-solving abilities. C5. Proven interpersonal and communication skills. C6. Numeracy and literacy skills. C7. A high level of accuracy and attention to detail. C8. Excellent organisational and time management skills and an ability to prioritise workload to meet competing demands. C9. Ability to work individually and as part of a team. Desirable: D1. Skills in high-throughput molecular cloning. Experience Essential: E1. Significant relevant work experience in a research laboratory. E2. Laboratory experience in core molecular biology techniques, including but not limited to DNA/RNA extraction, PCR, gel electrophoresis, bacterial culture and plasmid design and assembly. E3. Experience of understanding and interpreting the technical requirements of staff and students. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and has funding for up to 1st September 2029. This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries should be directed to Dr Abhinay Ramaprasad, Abhinay.Ramaprasad@glasgow.ac.uk. Closing Date: 19th November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Technician Glasgow Posted 4 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/administrative-assistant-6067065": {
      "title": "Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Administrative Assistant · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Administrative Assistant Administrative Assistant Back to jobs Administrative Assistant Job details Posted 22 October 2026 Salary Grade 5: £28,031 - £31,236 per annum Location Glasgow Reference 211275 Expiry 05 Nov 2026 22:45 Job description Please note this vacancy is open to current University of Glasgow employees only. Job Purpose To provide administrative support for the processes and procedures relating to students on Life Sciences undergraduate programmes. To provide an enquiry, information, advice and front-line support service to prospective, new and continuing students. To support staff and colleagues involved in teaching and teaching administration. To provide administrative support for delegated operational activities. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Provide administrative support for a wide range of operational and teaching functions, applying detailed knowledge of appropriate standard operating procedures and working practices, exercising initiative and judgement to ensure effective service delivery and suggesting and implementing improvements. Also facilitate and provide administrative support to members of the senior management team in the conduct of their duties. 2. Responsible for working closely with Directors of Education and Course Coordinators to provide administrative support for degree programmes and courses, taking advantage of existing systems and technology to ensure high levels of service delivery and data quality. Organise and clerk committees as required ensuring documentation is issued in a timely manner, monitor and follow-up action points to conclusion and maintain committee action logs. 3. Be proactive in supporting undergraduate students throughout registration and enrolment, accurately update student records as required, and ensure all students are fully registered and enrolled on appropriate courses to fulfil their Plan rules. Responsible for reviewing and instigating appropriate follow-up actions to ensure records are maintained and accurately updated. 4. Responsible for aspects of the assessment/examination process: contribute to the production of assessment and feedback calendars, prepare and upload papers for written and online assessments/exams, ensuring submission deadlines are met and grades uploaded. 5. Support student recruitment and conversion activities; attend Open Days; prepare programme and course induction meetings; prepare and upload to PIP student handbooks for undergraduate programmes and courses, assist with maintenance of course Moodle sites. 6.Support the work of the other members of the team, including sharing work during periods of peak workload and staff absence to ensure that full support is available. 7. Duties of the post outlined here are not exhaustive, the postholder will be expected to be co-operative and flexible and undertake such other duties as may be reasonably expected by management. The duties of the post will be subject to review in line with changing requirements, both internal and external. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 SCQF Level 7 (HNC, SVQ Level 3) or equivalent and relevant work experience  A2 Comprehensive knowledge of Microsoft Office 365 (Word, Excel, Teams and Outlook). Desirable: B1 Knowledge and understanding of University regulations relevant to undergraduate students. B2 Relevant experience of a Higher or Further Education environment. B3 Working knowledge of activities of other areas of the University that relate to support for undergraduate students. Skills Essential: C1 Excellent organisational skills and a proven ability to plan and prioritise work efficiently and multi-task whilst producing work of a high standard and to deadlines. C2 Excellent interpersonal skills with the ability to work as part of a team and deliver first-class support to colleagues and customers, demonstrating tact, diplomacy, and assertiveness to build excellent relationships. C3 Ability to work discreetly and sensitively, whilst maintaining confidentiality, when dealing with challenging situations. C4 Ability to create, maintain and interrogate spreadsheets. C5 High degree of accuracy and attention to detail. C6 Proven ability to work autonomously and proactively using own initiative to resolve. problems, consulting with senior colleagues as appropriate. C7 Proven ability to work with bespoke IT systems such as student records systems. C8 Ability to communicate effectively in writing to a high standard for the purposes of student documentation, correspondence, developing content for Moodle/web site. C9 Demonstrable experience in clerking committees and producing accurate minutes of meetings. Experience Essential: E1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills in a similar role providing high level support for staff and students. E2 Experience of organising and providing administrative support to (clerking) meetings/committees, producing accurate minutes to tight deadlines and follow through on actions. Demonstrable experience of working in a fast paced, customer focused environment. E3 Evidence of personal progression and development gained through work experience E4 Significant administrative experience demonstrating the ability to drive change to improve both systems and processes in place. E5 Experience of excellent service delivery and building customer relationships. Desirable: F1. Experience of analysing data and producing pivot tables, graphs and charts in Excel. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) to cover maternity leave for up to 12 months or until the substantive postholder returns. This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries should be directed to Mrs Tracy Wilson, Tracy.Wilson@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 5th November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Administrative Assistant Glasgow Posted 5 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/systems-support-analyst-crm-6067021": {
      "title": "Systems Support Analyst (CRM) · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Systems Support Analyst (CRM) · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Systems Support Analyst (CRM) Systems Support Analyst (CRM) Back to jobs Systems Support Analyst (CRM) Job details Posted 22 October 2026 Salary Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 211193 Expiry 12 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose Responsible for providing system configuration, user support and training, testing and user research for Salesforce, contributing to the implementation and continuous improvement of the University business solutions built on the Salesforce platform. Main Duties and Responsibilities To analyse system change requests and assess the impact of new system releases prior to implementation, ensuring any impact is considered and communicated to the appropriate staff/Schools/Colleges. Configure and customise Salesforce objects, fields, page layouts, record types, validation rules, workflows, and process automations to support university processes. Manage user setup, roles, profiles, permissions, and security settings to ensure appropriate access and data protection To provide specialist operational knowledge to users to mentor them in how to get the best from Salesforce. To resolve operational issues and queries independently through troubleshooting and advising on courses of action as appropriate. To carry out Salesforce system testing, identifying and capturing issues to support continuous improvement and efficiency. To support the development and delivery of training including user guides, knowledge base articles, and trouble-shooting guides. Develop and maintain reports and dashboards to provide actionable insights for stakeholders across the institution. To liaise with the Systems Development Manager and/or Project/Scrum Masters to create and add tasks to system backlogs as a result of issues raised via the helpdesk, testing or user research. To organise own workload within multiple projects by planning and prioritising own schedule and coordinating with other project team members in order to ensure that timescales and targets are met. To deal with sensitive information and data held for all University staff with a very high degree of confidentiality and discretion adhering to data protection legislation. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills through personal development and progression in a similar related role(s). Or: Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 8 (Scottish Vocational Qualification level 4, Higher National Diploma) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 An understanding of best practice in user support techniques, processes and principles, and helpdesk ticket systems. A3 An understanding of system testing and the logging of technology issues. A4 An understanding of the Salesforce CRM platform, it’s capabilities and best practices. Desirable: B1 Familiarity with Salesforce Education Cloud or Education Data Architecture (EDA) B2 A good understanding of FOI and DPA legislation. B3 Salesforce Certified Platform Administrator. B4 Salesforce Certified Marketing Cloud Engagement Administrator[GL1] . B5 Knowledge of reporting tools and data visualisation techniques. Skills Essential: C1 Proficiency in Salesforce configuration, including workflows, process builder, and lightning components. C2 Ability to manage and prioritise workload amongst many projects, meet tight deadlines and work under pressure. C3 A careful approach to work with particular attention to detail and high standards of accuracy. C4 Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills. C5 Ability to use initiative, work with minimal supervision and work as part of a team. C6 Proven ability to deliver IT training to users with varying skill sets. C7 Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. Experience Essential: E1 Experience working as a Salesforce Administrator in a complex organisational environment. E2 Experience in configuring and maintaining Salesforce to meet diverse user needs. E3 Experience of supporting and training users. E4 Experience of providing specialist advice and information to a wide range of audiences with differing levels of knowledge. Desirable: F1 Experience of working in the HE Sector. F2 Experience of working with agile IT teams. F3 Experience with Salesforce integration tools and APIs. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum. This post is full time and open ended. Closing date: 23:45 12th November 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit  University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website University of Glasgow's UKVI Website . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Systems Support Analyst (CRM) Glasgow Posted 5 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-impact-assistant-x2-6066949": {
      "title": "Research Impact Assistant x2",
      "description": "Research Impact Assistant x2 · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Impact Assistant x2 Research Impact Assistant x2 Back to jobs Research Impact Assistant x2 Job details Posted 22 October 2026 Salary £33,951 pa - £37,694 pa Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 210433 Expiry 05 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Please note this role is only open to current University of Glasgow employees and students Job purpose To support the administrative delivery of Research activities across the College of Social Sciences servicing a number of essential, operational research functions. To assist with the implementation of strategic goals and research exercises involving REF, impact, and engagement, and provide expert advice to key stakeholders. You will be required to support the collection of rigorous evidence of impact and increase the volume and quality of impact records held. Main duties and responsibilities: Working collaboratively with key stakeholders (e.g. Impact Officer, Engagement & Impact Manager, Dean of Research, Impact Case Study Leads) in co-ordinating engagement and knowledge exchange activities Provide supervisory support to the Engagement & Impact, contributing to the delivery of an effective service, covering areas such as REF, impact and engagement and income tracking support. Contributing to the operational aspects of Impact Case Studies, providing assistance in monitoring progress and maintaining the impact tracker database; and overseeing the collection of data to support the development of the environment statement. Build and maintain up to date knowledge of the REF requirements, and related regulations to facilitate responses to high level enquiries. Contribute to the development of impact report assisting in the identification, recording, and monitoring of impact and needs assessment projects, providing operational support to achieve impact actions. Responsible for the development of a database to record academic skills and expertise, facilitating connections with industry. Act as an administrative contact for academic staff, providing advice in relation to research and knowledge exchange, including engagement activities where applicable. Provide high quality support to senior academic and professional services staff in relation to impact mapping and tracking. Contribute to reporting activities by overseeing the collection, preparation, and presentation of research data, preparing materials and evidence as required. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the team. Engage in reasonable professional development activities as appropriate. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge & Qualifications Essential A1 Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 8 (Scottish Vocational Qualification level 4, Higher National Diploma) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills through personal development and progression in a similar related role(s). A3 Understanding of knowledge exchange and research impact within the social sciences Desirable B1 Up-to-date knowledge and awareness of current issues of research within UK HEIs. Skills Essential C1 Ability to work proactively and independently to prioritise, take appropriate initiative. C2 Able to maintain high standards of numerical and data accuracy, with attention to detail and professionalism. C3 Excellent oral and written communication skills with the ability to interact effectively with stakeholders at all levels. C4 Ability to use initiative and judgement to resolve problems independently. C5 Ability to work flexibly and adapt to changing environments. Experience Essential E1 High proficiency in use of IT platforms (e.g., Microsoft packages excel, work, outlook) and databases. E2 Experience of analysing and manipulating complex data Desirable F1 Experience of working in a research environment. F2 Experience public engagement activities and/or writing about research impact F3 Experience of working on research projects involving knowledge exchange and impact Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata. This post is part time (20 hours per week) and fixed term until the 31st of July 2027 Closing date: 23:45 5th of November 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Research Impact Assistant x2 Glasgow Posted 5 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/lecturer-in-human-resource-management-and-organisational-behaviour-r-and-t-track-6066861": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour (R&T Track)",
      "description": "Lecturer in Human Resource Management and · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Lecturer in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour (R&T Track) Lecturer in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour (R&T Track) Back to jobs Lecturer in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour (R&T Track) Job details Posted 22 October 2026 Salary Grade 7/8: £41,064 - £46,049/£50,253 - £58,225 per annum End date 19 November 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210194 Expiry 19 Nov 2026 22:45 Job description Job Advert The Adam Smith Business School seeks a Lecturer to contribute research and teaching in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour as part of its Management subject. A specialism in equalities and diversity, including around neurodiversity, is of particular interest. The individual will contribute their emerging research programme and support the research activities and culture in the Work, Organisations, and Political Economy cluster. They will deliver and coordinate learning, teaching and associated activities such as assessment, dissertation supervision, quality assurance and delivering an excellent student experience across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. At Grade 8, the role holder will make a substantial and positive contribution to programmes in the Management subject area, and occasionally across the School, by contributing to their research programme and collaborating with colleagues. They will support an excellent student experience by leading and contributing to teaching and assessment, and by providing academic leadership and management associated with undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. The successful candidate for this post will be enrolled onto the University’s Early Career Development Programme (ECDP). This will provide you as an early career academic staff member to be developed and supported over a specified timeframe to facilitate the advancement of your academic career.  Information on the programme can be found here . Job Purpose To undertake research of international excellence and contribute to knowledge exchange activities relative to the discipline, contribute to learning and teaching on agreed programmes and undertake administration and service activities in line with the School/College’s strategic objectives. For appointment at Grade 8 , a substantial contribution will include leading high quality research activities and delivering, organising and reviewing key areas of teaching and learning within the School. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Develop and progress individual/joint research projects of international standard in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour with the support of a mentor, as required. 2. Write up research findings/outcomes for publication in leading journals for dissemination as appropriate, identifying potential sources and contributing to funding applications as an individual or team member. 3. Contribute fully to developing and enhancing the research profile of the School/College, including establishing a track record of high-quality publications. 4. Contribute to the planning, organisation and delivery of teaching on undergraduate and/or postgraduate courses within Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour and, where appropriate, the broader Management subject in accordance with the subject’s priorities including accepting responsibilities as course co-ordinator for one or more courses. 5. Contribute to the ongoing development and design of the curriculum in a manner that supports a research-led and scholarly approach to student learning and assessment in the discipline and/or profession. 6. Participate fully in examination and other assessment processes, as appropriate, using a variety of methods and techniques and to provide effective, timely and appropriate feedback to students to support their learning. 7. Contribute to the development of theory and practice in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour. 8. Supervise individual student projects at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, and to contribute to the supervision and training of postgraduate research students, as appropriate, to ensure their effective development 9. Take on administrative or management roles within the Management subject/School as assigned by the Head of School. 10. Engage in professional development as appropriate 11. To contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategy: Glasgow 2036 – World Changing Glasgow – University for the World: https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/strategy/ For appointment at Grade 8: In addition to the duties and responsibilities above: 1. Pursue and deliver independent research proposals and/or collaborative research projects, project managing research activities, supervising and taking responsibility for the research team. Securing external resources to support research individually and/or jointly, as appropriate to the subject area. 2. Make a substantial contribution to developing and enhancing the research profile of the School/College. Establishing and leading research collaborations with appropriate individuals and groups within the University, including multi-disciplinary links, and developing external research collaborations both nationally and internationally, to ensure that research activities are at the forefront of the field. 3. Take a lead in the on-going development and design of the curriculum in a manner which supports a research-led and scholarly approach to student learning and assessment, making a substantial contribution to the teaching and learning activities within the School. 4. Develop new and effective approaches to the use of learning technologies to support and enhance course delivery, organisation, feedback and assessment. 5. Develop a growing portfolio of research outputs and activities and support early career staff in developing their own portfolio. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1: Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) or equivalent professional and/or teaching experience in a similar or related role. A2: Sufficient breadth and/or depth of knowledge in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour and of research/teaching methods and techniques to work within the Management subject area. A3: Emerging national/international research profile in the Management subject area. For Appointment at Grade 8: A4: Extensive breadth and depth of knowledge in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour to develop research/teaching programmes/methodologies/learning support. A5: Established reputation in research and teaching, including an international research profile in the Management subject area. A6: Membership of relevant professional body, where appropriate. Desirable: B1: Expertise in human resource development and equality, diversity and inclusion, and international human resource management. Skills Essential: C1: Ability to communicate material of a specialist or highly technical nature and to be effective both orally and in writing at a level appropriate to the activity. C2: Ability to engage the interest and enthusiasm of students to inspire learning and to provide pastoral care to students within the Management subject area. C3: Ability to apply policy, practice and procedures including broader University/sector/external (e.g. commercial) awareness. C4: Ability to work on own initiative to contribute to the research and teaching activities within the Management subject area and wider School environment. C5: Effective organisation skills to manage time and prioritise research, teaching and administrative responsibilities as appropriate. For Appointment at Grade 8: C6: Ability to communicate complex or conceptual ideas to those with limited knowledge and understanding, and developing early career academic colleagues. C7: Ability to contribute to broader leadership and management activities along with an awareness of current and future priorities of the University/College/School/Service. C8: Ability to collaborate with colleagues in other disciplines and/or institutions in developing research. Experience Essential: E1: Experience of planning and progressing work activities within professional guidelines or organisational policy, applying initiative and independent judgement. E2: Developing track record of research outputs compatible with enhancing the School’s research profile in accordance with School objectives. For Appointment at Grade 8: E3: Established track record of research outputs and of applying for, and securing, external research funding compatible with enhancing the School’s research profile in accordance with School objectives. E4: Track record of scholarship and development and delivery of teaching, and proven ability to develop and devise teaching programmes, techniques and methods. E5: Experience of planning and progressing work activities within professional guidelines or organisational policy, applying initiative and independent judgement. E6: Experience of supervision of students at honours or postgraduate level E7: Track record of continuous engagement with current practice and developing knowledge. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7/8, £41,064 - £46,049/£50,253 - £58,225 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and fixed term for up to 4 years from start date. Informal enquiries can be directed to Marjana Johansson, Cluster Lead, at Marjana.Johansson@glasgow.ac.uk  or John Finch, Professor of Marketing, John.Finch@glasgow.ac.uk All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website: https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/pod/visasimmigration/skilledworkervisa/ For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date: 23:45 on Thursday 19th November 2026 Apply Now Save this job Lecturer in Human Resource Management and Organisational Behaviour (R&T Track) Glasgow Posted 5 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/sgsah-programme-and-engagement-coordinator-6066525": {
      "title": "(SGSAH) Programme and Engagement Coordinator",
      "description": "(SGSAH) Programme and Engagement Coordinator · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs (SGSAH) Programme and Engagement Coordinator (SGSAH) Programme and Engagement Coordinator Back to jobs (SGSAH) Programme and Engagement Coordinator Job details Posted 21 October 2026 Salary Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 210773 Expiry 04 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose The postholder will provide programme, communications and engagement support across SGSAH’s national consortium of 17 Higher Education Institutions (HEIs) and wider partner network, including SGSAH’s role as the AHRC Doctoral Landscape Award (DLA) Scotland Hub.  Working as part of a small national team based within the University of Glasgow, the postholder will support the coordination and delivery of selected SGSAH programmes, including DLA Hub and Partnership PHD activity, communications, events and researcher engagement, working to agreed procedures, timelines and priorities. The role will include coordinating programme communications and digital content, supporting partnership PhD programmes and funding-call administration, maintaining accurate information and records, supporting allocated events, alumni and DLA Hub activity, as well as  contributing information and evidence for monitoring and evaluation. Reporting to the PGR Programme and Operations Manager, the postholder will work closely with the Partnerships and Placements Manager and wider SGSAH team to support consistent, timely and accessible delivery across SGSAH programmes and activities. The reporting line reflects the temporary nature of the post and is intended to provide consistent day-to-day management and support across the working week, while maintaining close working links with the Partnerships and Placements Manager on relevant areas of delivery. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Programme, DLA Hub and Partnership PhD Engagement and Support Take responsibility for the day-to-day coordination and administration of allocated SGSAH programmes and activities, including DLA Hub, researcher development, Partnership PhDs and engagement activity. Support the administration and communication of programme and studentship opportunities and calls, including preparing and publishing information, coordinating timelines, maintaining records, preparing documentation for review or selection processes and communicating outcomes. Coordinate allocated DLA Hub activity, including programme administration, communications, training and event support, participant information and follow-up. Coordinate the operational administration of Partnership PhD activity, including calls, proposal circulation, review and panel arrangements, routine communications and maintenance of project and partner records. Liaise with SGSAH member HEIs, DLA Hub participating HEIs, SGSAH Directorate, supervisory teams, partner organisations, speakers, contributors and doctoral researchers to coordinate information, actions and deadlines and support effective delivery. Maintain accurate, accessible and up-to-date information, guidance, deadlines and participant records across SGSAH systems and communications channels. Respond to programme-specific enquiries from doctoral researchers, HEI colleagues and partners, providing clear information in accordance with agreed guidance and referring contractual, financial, policy, eligibility or other complex or exceptional matters appropriately. Collect and collate participation data, feedback and other evidence to support monitoring and evaluation of SGSAH and DLA Hub activity. Contribute to the review and improvement of programme administration and engagement processes to support efficient, consistent and accessible delivery. 2. Communications and Digital Engagement Coordinate and deliver agreed communications activity across SGSAH, including newsletters, website content, news items, programme guidance, application information, event communications and stakeholder updates. Maintain SGSAH's website and digital channels, ensuring information is accurate, current, accessible and updated within agreed timescales. Coordinate SGSAH's use of social media and other digital channels to promote doctoral training, DLA Hub and SFC-funded activity, funding opportunities, events, partnership activity and doctoral researcher achievements. Implement agreed communications priorities and campaigns, ensuring clear and consistent messaging for doctoral researchers, member HEIs, partners and wider audiences. Create or coordinate appropriate digital and visual content and liaise with internal or external suppliers where required. Support agreed alumni engagement activity, including communications, maintaining contact information and promoting opportunities for alumni involvement in SGSAH activity. 3. Events and Researcher Development Coordinate the planning and delivery of allocated SGSAH training, events and researcher development activity, including participant communications, registrations, logistics and follow-up. Liaise with member HEIs, external partners, speakers, venues, suppliers and participants to support delivery of allocated events and activities. Maintain accurate schedules, participant information and activity records for allocated activity and ensure agreed actions and deadlines are met. Collect and collate feedback, participation data and evaluation information for allocated programmes and events. 4. Other reasonable duties Undertake other reasonable duties appropriate to the level and nature of the post as assigned by the PGR Programme and Operations Manager, Partnerships and Placements Manager and the SGSAH Directorate. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 7 (Advanced Higher/Scottish Vocational Qualification level 3/Higher National Certificate) or equivalent, AND experience of personal development in a similar role, OR the ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills in a similar role. A2 Knowledge and understanding of effective programme, event or project administration and the importance of accurate, timely information and records. A3 Knowledge and understanding of effective digital engagement, including creating and adapting content for web, email, social and other online channels. A4 Understanding how to create clear, accessible and audience-appropriate content across different formats and channels. Desirable: B1 Knowledge or experience of higher education, postgraduate research, arts and humanities, or the cultural, creative or third sectors. B2 Knowledge or experience of partnership, collaborative studentship, researcher development or alumni engagement activity. B3 Awareness of data protection, accessibility and information governance requirements, including GDPR. Skills Essential: C1 Strong organisational and coordination skills, with the ability to manage multiple strands of programme, event and engagement activity, prioritising competing deadlines. C2 Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to create clear, engaging and accessible content. C3 Ability to use judgement and initiative to resolve routine issues, respond constructively to changing circumstances and recognise when matters should be escalated. C4 Ability to build and maintain effective working relationships. C5 Ability to work confidently and collaboratively within a small team, taking ownership of allocated activity while contributing flexibly to wider programme delivery. C6 Confidence using digital platforms, systems and online channels to support communications, programme administration and engagement activity. Desirable: D1 Experience of using content management, application management, CRM or comparable administrative systems. Experience Essential: E1 Experience coordinating programmes, projects, events or engagement activity in a professional environment. E2 Experience producing clear and engaging communications or content for different audiences and channels. E3 Experience coordinating activity involving multiple stakeholders, partners or contributors, including managing information, actions and deadlines. E4 Experience maintaining accurate programme, participant, application or stakeholder information using digital systems. E5 Experience contributing to monitoring, evaluation or reporting activity, including gathering, collating and presenting information. Desirable: F1 Experience working in higher education, postgraduate research, arts and humanities, cultural, creative or third-sector environments. F2 Experience supporting collaborative programmes involving multiple institutions, partners or stakeholder groups. F3 Experience using content management, CRM, application-management, event-management or comparable digital systems. F4 Experience supporting alumni, community or stakeholder engagement activity. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time and fixed term to cover maternity leave for up to 12 months or until the substantive postholder returns. Please Note: If you are a current employee of the University of Glasgow, please be advised that the University would consider an internal secondment opportunity for this post. University of Glasgow applicants should seek clearance for release for internal secondment from their line manager before making a formal application. This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/pod/visasimmigration/ As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date : 4 November 2026 @23:45 Apply Now Save this job (SGSAH) Programme and Engagement Coordinator Glasgow Posted 5 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/receivables-lead-6056027": {
      "title": "Receivables Lead · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Receivables Lead · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Receivables Lead Receivables Lead Back to jobs Receivables Lead Job details Posted 09 October 2026 Salary Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum Location Glasgow Reference 210295 Expiry 01 Nov 2026 22:45 Job description Please note this vacancy is only open to current contracted University of Glasgow employees. Job Purpose Provide comprehensive finance operational support and advice to receivables team, University colleagues, students, and customers. Act as the main point of contact between UofG staff and receivables team to resolve issues and provide proactive and effective service delivery. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1.      Support Receivables Manager and Receivables Team Lead in the delivery of service excellence to students, customers, and internal colleagues, providing advice on resourcing and workload to support forward planning to meet KPIs and SLAs. 2.      Provide advice, guidance and support to the team and where necessary process complex financial transactions accurately, including journals and invoices, ensuring adherence to financial requirements and controls, and delivering within team KPIs and SLAs. 3.      Support the delivery of a strong financial control environment across the team, ensuring team members and colleagues adhere to financial guidelines, policies, and procedures. 4.      Lead on the provision of training on policies, procedures, and systems for new and existing members of staff, ensuring consistency in approach and delivery. 5.      Contribute to the documentation, review, and validation of all standard operating procedures to ensure they up to date and fit for purpose. 6.      Monitor daily workloads and assign tasks based on priorities and SLAs, ensuring all daily processing tasks are completed in an accurate and timely manner, highlighting any potential issues. 7.      Monitor, review, and resolve all exceptions through reporting, completion of balance sheet controls account or bank account reconciling items, within agreed KPIs. 8.      To review, monitor and update master data changes across systems, ensuring appropriate controls and processes are adhered to. 9.      To be the first point of contact to provide clear financial advice and guidance on processes relating to the receivables team, including resolving issues, as required. 10.   Analyse and document key financial information and activities, to produce straightforward reporting, identifying trends and patterns. 11.   Build relationships and maintain an effective network of communication with relevant colleagues across key stakeholders both within Finance Operations and across the university, contributing to process improvements as appropriate. 12.   Support the refund and debt write off approvals process through assigned tasks within the team delegated authority. 13.   Contribute to the development, testing and implementation of systems and procedures to ensure continuous improvement in the performance of the Finance Operations Service. 14.   Assist with ad hoc projects and other relevant duties as required relating to the Finance Operations team. 15.   Undertake and participate in continued professional development activities. 16.   Contribute to a culture of service excellence and control to delivery against key performance indicators, service level agreements and finance strategies. 17.   Maintain technical knowledge through educational workshops, conferences, keeping up to date with current developments, governance and compliance requirements and contribution to policies and procedures, considering any legislation. Knowledge/Qualifications, Skills, Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills in a similar role. Or Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework Level 7 (Advanced Higher / Scottish Vocational Qualification Level 3, Higher National Certificate) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 Detailed knowledge and understanding of maintaining financial data or Master data in office applications, financial or data management systems. A3 Detailed knowledge of financial processes and systems. Desirable: B1 Knowledge and awareness of financial sanctions, anti-money laundering guidelines, fraud prevention measures, for example know your payer / supplier / customer checks. B2 Knowledge of GDPR and information privacy regulations. Skills Essential: C1 Strong oral and written communication skills, with proven interpersonal skills dealing with a wide range of stakeholders. C2 Ability to work closely with, and influence, other teams. C3 Excellent organisational, time management and organisation skills. C4 Ability to learn and adapt to new systems. C5 Excellent IT skills, specifically highly proficient in Excel, Word, and networked systems. C6 Good analytical and problem-solving skills. C7 Excellent attention to detail and ability to work with high degree of numerical and data accuracy. C8 Initiative and judgement to resolve problems independently. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of processing, manipulating, and analysing data. E2 Experience in using customer relationship management (CRM) systems (i.e. Ivanti). E3 Experience of supervising the work of others. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours p/w) and open ended. Closing date: 1 November 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Receivables Lead Glasgow Posted 18 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
