    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/centre-for-data-science-and-ai-communications-and-engagement-coordinator-6052320": {
      "title": "Centre for Data Science and AI Communications and Engagement Coordinator",
      "description": "Centre for Data Science and AI Communications · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Centre for Data Science and AI Communications and Engagement Coordinator Centre for Data Science and AI Communications and Engagement Coordinator Back to jobs Centre for Data Science and AI Communications and Engagement Coordinator Job details Posted 06 October 2026 Salary Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Location Glasgow Reference 204074 Expiry 02 Nov 2026 22:45 Job description Please note this vacancy is only open to current contracted University of Glasgow employees. Job Purpose Reporting to the Centre for Data Science and AI Manager, the Communications and Engagement Lead will lead on developing and delivering a comprehensive and effective engagement and communication activities for the Centre. This role will provide support for the creation of effective internal communication plans, working closely with the projects on a day-to-day basis. Actively engage with multiple stakeholders within the University and be responsible for the creation and deployment of a variety of communication assets and approaches, delivered in a targeted way via multiple channels, and compliant with University of Glasgow brand guidelines and values framework. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Provide clear and practical communications advice to the Centre for Data Science and AI Team. Leading on developing and implementing strategic communications and engagement plans that effectively target internal and external audiences and highlight the breadth and depth of AI and data science activities and impact, engaging colleagues and partners in the programme. 2. Work in collaboration with the central communications team to manage the coordination, creation and delivery of external communications related to UofG-led AI and data science related projects and initiatives. 3. Maintain the Centre for Data Science and AI website, ensuring that content is up to date and regularly refreshed. Maintain Centre-related content across the University web pages, ensuring colleagues have relevant and up-to-date information about the programme and activities. 4. Establish and manage a portfolio of Centre social media channels, developing content plans and distributing high-quality content to promote programme activity. Work in collaboration with colleagues to ensure other relevant social media channels amplify relevant Centre programme news and activities. 5. Lead on creating and commissioning relevant communications content, sourcing images and collating and/or writing copy. Collateral may include email newsletters, case studies, internal announcements, publications, presentations, printed materials. 6. Organise and supervise Centre communication interns/fellows and engagement activities as required; encouraging high-quality community generated communication content including photography, case studies, blogs and videos. 7. Lead on the organisation of engagement events and workshops. Creating and coordinating communications collateral in support of the engagement activities. 8. Analyse and evaluate the effectiveness of communication strategies and plans using qualitative and quantitative metrics. Use this data to prepare and deliver progress reports to key stakeholders, advising on success criteria and key metrics, ensuring this data informs communication plans and decision-making. 9. Establish and maintain effective internal relationships through collaborative working to ensure the Centre for Data Science and AI narrative is amplified and represented across a range of strategic communications projects/campaigns, business development opportunities, shared initiatives and external partnership projects. This will involve working across the Research Services Directorate, External Relations, Government Relations, Colleges, and Schools. 10. As a staff member of the Research Services Directorate, you will work in collaboration with colleagues on agreed continuous improvement projects. You will bring your demonstrable communications knowledge and practical skills to these projects, which ensure the efficient and effective operations of the Directorate. 11. Perform other duties as requested, consistent with the grade level of the position and agreeing appropriate prioritisation of work with line management or other relevant stakeholders as needed. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills through personal development and progression in a similar role. Or: Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 8 (Scottish Vocational Qualification Level 4, Higher National Diploma) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 Detailed knowledge and proven track record in the delivery of internal communications and engagement with demonstrable success. A3 Detailed knowledge and experience working in an agile project-based environment. Desirable: B1 Membership of an appropriate professional body, either relating to events, engagement or communication. Skills Essential: C1 Effective communication skills with excellent writing ability and demonstrable evidence of message delivery for different audiences - print, digital, social media, broadcast, and internal engagement. C2 Excellent interpersonal skills, including diplomacy and discretion, and the ability to liaise with staff and external individuals at any level of seniority. C3 Ability to understand, interpret and summarise complex issues and research across a broad range of disciplines. C4 Initiative, creativity, energy, and enthusiasm, as well as excellent time management and organisational skills, with the ability to define priorities and work flexibly and effectively under pressure to meet demanding (and sometimes conflicting) deadlines. C5 Effective web management skills with an ability to effectively manage social media channels. C6 Ability to work collaboratively and to take the lead when required with external funders and partner institutions on complex engagement activities and events. C7 High degree of initiative, responsibility and self-motivation, and a professional and proactive approach to problem solving. Experience Essential: E1 Relevant experience in a similar role, producing communication and engagement plans and content. E2 Relevant event / engagement experience both in person and virtually that achieves high-quality experiences and outcomes for those participating. E3 Experience of managing / supervising the work of others. E4 Relevant experience of contributing to, designing and implementing communication plans for internal and external audiences. E5 Experience of working collaboratively with colleagues in other professional teams. E6 A track record of credible engagement and working with senior leaders. E7 Clear and evidenced experience of developing, producing, sharing, and embedding high quality written, visual, and audio-visual materials for mainstream, online and social media audiences. E8 Experience of briefing and managing external suppliers of creative services such as design agencies and videographers. Desirable: F1 Experience of organising events for an international audience and of working across cultural differences and challenges, including events that target underserved or marginalised communities. F2 Experience of working in the Higher Education sector. F3 Experience of using Canva. F4 Experience of using the T4 Content Management System. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum, pro rata. This post is part time (17.5 hours p/w) with funding to 28 February 2029. Shifts will be 2.5 days over Monday to Friday. Days to be agreed with the successful candidate. Closing date: 2 November 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Centre for Data Science and AI Communications and Engagement Coordinator Glasgow Posted 21 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/biochemistry-technician-6046186": {
      "title": "Biochemistry Technician",
      "description": "Biochemistry Technician · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Biochemistry Technician Biochemistry Technician Back to jobs Biochemistry Technician Job details Posted 02 October 2026 Salary Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Technical And Specialist Reference 209553 Expiry 05 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To provide in-depth technical expertise in the College of Science and Engineering supporting world leading research and undergraduate and post graduate training and teaching. The role provides expert technical advice on the creation, set up and deployment of technical resources to aid the delivery of research and teaching objectives and on the operation and maintenance of analytical equipment. The role will play a critical role in delivering support across research lab activities including data analysis, sample preparation and processing, and the safe handling, use, and disposal of hazardous substances. The postholder will apply a range of laboratory, scientific, and chemical skills to support a range of technical services in line with the College Strategy. Main Duties and Responsibilities Contribute specialist technical expertise to the day-to-day operation and continuous improvement of laboratory facilities across multiple buildings within the College, ensuring an efficient and effective service for academic and student users within research laboratories, contributing to the successful delivery of research objectives. Provide technical knowledge, support, solutions, and advice on chromatography and spectroscopy systems and related other specialist laboratory equipment.  Support the development, implementation and validation of analytical methods, procedures and techniques for scientific equipment. Diagnose faults, troubleshoot operational issues, undertake routine maintenance, and liaise with service providers to maintain equipment performance. Design, plan and coordinate technical activities and workflows to meet the requirements of service users within agreed timescales and priorities. Deliver training, instruction and user guidance on the safe and effective application and operation of scientific equipment, supporting the development of user competence and good laboratory practice. In liaison with relevant senior staff, monitor technical standards and quality requirements, recommending, implementing and evaluating improvements to laboratory procedures, protocols and working practices. Collect, process, analyse and interpret technical data using established procedures, ensuring accurate record keeping, database maintenance, and clear presentation of findings. Manage laboratory stocks, consumables and specialist materials, including the preparation of samples and reagents where required. Source and purchase equipment, consumables and supplies within delegated budgets. Support the safe and efficient operation of laboratory workspaces and facilities, implementing University and Health and Safety Executive requirements, assisting with risk assessments and COSHH documentation, and conducting laboratory inductions for new users. Coordinate solvent, chemical and hazardous waste streams, ensuring compliance with relevant legislation and best practice. Support the introduction, storage and safe use of hazardous substances within College laboratories. Provide support for undergraduate teaching laboratories where required by preparing equipment and materials to facilitate practical teaching activities. Collaborate with colleagues and participate in team meetings and discussions and contribute to the wider College/Institute/School activities as required, including the Technician Commitment key themes. https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/technicians/ Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience. Knowledge, Qualifications Essential: A1 Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 7 [Advanced Higher / Scottish vocational Qualification level 3, Higher National Certificate] or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 Demonstrable technical knowledge and practical expertise in biochemistry or biology laboratory operations. A3 Detailed knowledge and awareness of technical support responsibilities within a chemistry laboratory including implementation and development of standard operating procedures, quality control, and safety compliance (e.g., COSHH and Risk Assessments). Desirable: B1 Degree in Chemistry, Biochemistry or similar subjects. B2 Knowledge of biochemistry or molecular biology principles, including the safe handling of biological materials and an understanding of biological risk management and containment practices. Skills Essential: C1 Specialist technical skill in performing, instructing and supervising activities in a chemistry or bio-chemistry lab including the safe handling, use, and disposal of chemicals. C2 Demonstrable skills in operating and maintaining analytical equipment such as Gas/Liquid/Ion Chromatography, mass spectrometry, FTIR spectroscopy, and/or Thermogravimetric Analysis. C3 Well-developed analytical and problem-solving capability, including high degree of accuracy and attention to detail. C4 Ability to train and supervise staff and other internal and external service users. C5 Proven interpersonal, communication and teamworking skills with ability to build relationships across multiple disciplines. C6 Strong time management skills with ability to undertake assigned tasks and prioritise work according to the demands of busy research laboratories, delivering in a timely manner and acceptable standard. C7 Significant IT skills, including proficient user of relevant specialist and standard software packages. C8 Ability to plan and prioritise workload to meet competing demands. C9 Ability to communicate clearly orally and in writing to ensure effective reporting and customer service. C10 Advanced technical skills in analytical chemistry techniques, including chromatography and spectroscopy systems. Desirable: D1 Skills in preparing organic and inorganic samples for analysis. Experience Essential: E1 Significant relevant work experience within a similar environment, including evidence of previous professional development. E2 Experience of understanding, prioritising and responding to the technical requirements of service users, ensuring the effective delivery of technical support to staff and students in a busy laboratory environment. E3 Experience of working in a busy scientific lab including maintaining stock and equipment, and procurement. E4 Evidence of equipment maintenance, repairs and servicing. Desirable: F1 Experience working in a multi-disciplinary science laboratory. F2 Experience working across multiple labs and buildings. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours p/w) and open ended. Closing date: 6 November 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Biochemistry Technician Glasgow Posted 25 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/project-manager-6045005": {
      "title": "Project Manager · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Project Manager · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Project Manager Project Manager Back to jobs Project Manager Job details Posted 01 October 2026 Salary £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 209434 Expiry 04 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose This role represents an exciting opportunity to provide high level and specialist project management and administrative service. Based in the School for Social and Political Sciences, this role will work across two major research programmes: Changing Realities and Safety Nets. Changing Realities is a major, busy participatory research project working with over 200 parents and carers on a low-income from all four nations of the UK. The project also partners with national charity, Child Poverty Action Group, and includes impact and policymaking activities at the highest levels. Safety Nets is the UK’s first examination of the relationship between social security and devolution in the UK, and entails a partnership of seven universities, Resolution Foundation and Child Poverty Action Group.) The role is to support the PI (Ruth Patrick) in coordinating both projects, and making sure that they achieving key deliverables, and to time. The postholder will also be responsible for ensuring good and effective internal and external communication; and in supporting the projects to regularly review and improve their practice. The successful candidate will have significant experience in providing a full range of project management functions, including stakeholder management, higher-level administrative services, including financial and multi-site team coordination for higher education, government and/or Third Sector projects involving co-production and participatory approaches. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work independently and as part of a team are requirements for the job. The role will be part of the College of Social Sciences Research Support Office and will report into the College Research Operations Manager (Angela Cowan) but will work closely with internal and external colleagues on Changing Realities and Safety Nets. Main Duties and Responsibilities Take a lead role, in collaboration with the research teams to plan, monitor and review the strategic aims, objectives and key milestones of the Changing Realities and Safety Nets research programmes. Plan and coordinate a series of activities, meetings and workshops with diverse groups of participants from multiple policy sectors. Manage the logistics of the workshops, coordinating the facilitators and managing all data and materials that are required or generated during these events. Managing internal and external relationships relevant to the projects. Line Management and workload allocation of administrative staff. Working with College Research Support Office to manage, disburse, monitor and report on budget and resources, including co-ordinating with partners and funders. Engage in strategic business planning processes and the management of project work by providing specialist administrative support to the research teams and supplying relevant information and statistical analysis as required. Establish and sustain project management processes and key project documents (e.g. report writing, risk logs) to ensure a record of contacts, events, outputs, and other project activities are kept up-to-date, accessible and accurate. Take a key role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues and external stakeholders, including at the highest of levels. Plan, promote and manage logistics for virtual and in-person meetings and events and other research and engagement activities run by the projects, instigating and monitoring appropriate follow-up actions to ensure quality enhancement. Lead on the implementation of both projects’ communication strategies, for example maintaining the projects’ web and social media presence, supporting with media releases, producing briefings, blogs, podcasts, explainer videos and similar material,  and providing promotional content to other channels such as the University’s and Council’s Marketing teams, as required by the research team. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by Line Manager or project director. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan, World Changers Together. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 Either; Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 9 (Ordinary Degree, Scottish Vocational Qualification level 4), or equivalent (including professional accreditation with relevant formal training), and experience of personal development in a similar or related role(s), Or: Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary  knowledge and skills in a similar or number of different roles. A2 Excellent knowledge of a range of project management tools and approaches that can be used to support higher education-led research projects. A3 Knowledge and understanding of the UK and devolved policymaking context. A4 Excellent knowledge and understanding of project and event planning and coordination. Desirable B1 Some familiarity with poverty and social security policy landscape. B2 Formal programme management qualification, e.g. PRINCE2 (or working towards this). Skills Essential C1 Excellent verbal and written communication skills, and ability to communicate effectively with diverse technical and lay audiences using a range of media. C2 Excellent interpersonal skills, including the ability to effectively communicate, work with and engage with researchers, policy makers, members of the public and community groups. C3 Proven analytical, influencing and problem-solving capability. C4 Advanced IT skills, including using online platforms to host events, workshops, or interactive sessions, report and presentation writing, and skills for web editing using a content management system. C5 Proven skills with using social media to promote projects and engage stakeholders and the wider public. C6 Ability to work flexibly and adapt to changing environments. C7 Ability to work across sectoral boundaries with a wide range of professions and disciplines representing academia, local authorities and other sectors relevant to the project. C8 Strong problem-solving skills, especially when working with multiple partners, and in a time-sensitive way. Experience Essential E1 Experience of all aspects of research communication and events organisation. E2 Experience of dealing with processes and procedures for partnership working and public engagement. E3 Experience of  planning, co-ordinating, monitoring and recording of research activities and their impact. E4 Line management experience E5 Experience of using corporate financial and administrative business systems E6 Experience of budgetary management and monitoring and ability to monitor budget and produce financial reporting as per the requirements of the funder and the university Desirable F1 Experience of social media communication strategies. F2 Experience of policymaking stakeholder engagement Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time and is fixed term until the 31st August 2027 Closing date: 23:45 4th November 15th October 2026 The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Project Manager Glasgow Posted 26 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
