    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/grounds-operative-6040886": {
      "title": "Grounds Operative · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Grounds Operative · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Grounds Operative Grounds Operative Back to jobs Grounds Operative Job details Posted 28 September 2026 Salary Grade 4, £25,804 - £27,319 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 208834 Expiry 09 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose The role holder will be responsible for maintaining, preparing and delivering a year-round programme of maintenance and renovation to the outdoor facilities and surrounding areas, at Garscube Sports Complex. They will maintaining the excellent UofG facilities and equipment while sustaining a safe and welcoming environment. Main Duties & Responsibilities Maintain a multi-sports complex comprising of six grass pitches, two synthetic pitches, three outdoor synthetic tennis courts, one cricket outfield with seven wickets, as well a variety of non-sports related gardens and grounds areas. Deliver an outstanding customer experience by applying knowledge and experience in delivering playing surfaces for all level of users. Responsible for arboriculture and horticulture and have an understanding of environmental considerations, sustainability and the surrounding eco-system. Responsible for renovating natural grass pitch surfaces and nurturing seedlings through stressful periods, and implement corrective measures to control and protect. Operate a range of specialist driving equipment and machinery and hold the necessary qualifications and experience. Responsible for chemical spray application (PA1 & PA6 certification). Undertake tree pruning and chipping and hold current LANTRA qualifications. Ability to provide soil analysis and calibrate materials to undertake grounds work throughout the year, contributing to best value and improved sustainability. In conjunction with the Lead Grounds person create and develop work schedules and renovations programme. Erect and dismantle sports equipment for team use and carrying and carry out maintenance checks and the reporting of faults. Ability to work safely as part of a team or individually taking ownership of tasks to ensure all H&S guidelines are being followed. Take ownership of University machinery and equipment (including items for personal use), including the maintenance and reporting of faults. Knowledge of the relevant sport’s National Governing Body guidance and field layout requirements and methods. Deputise for Lead Grounds person, when required. Be responsible for matters relating to Health, Safety and Compliance, using safe working practices at all times, reporting any breaches, near misses or defects. Clear and treat snow and ice during inclement weather to ensure that the paths and roads are accessible to prevent disruption to University of Glasgow Sport’s business continuity. Ability to carry out an environmental assessment prior to applying chemicals or fertilisers to limit damage caused by misuse, drift, or faulty machinery to always ensure safe application to protect the environment plants insect’s species, animals, and persons. Ensure compliance with relevant HSE and industry legislation during all work activities, as detailed within the site’s emergency plan and operating procedures. Assist in co-ordination with emergency services, customers and Duty Managers in the event of an incident, as detailed within the site’s emergency plan and operating procedures. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge and Qualifications Essential: A1 Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework Level 4 in English and Mathematics (National 4) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role or; ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills through personal development and progression in a similar related role(s). A2 Knowledge and understanding in green keeping, fine turf management or equivalent Professional Development awards. A3 Knowledge and understanding to repair natural grass pitch surfaces before and after game time/training sessions or events to ensure optimum recovery. A4 Full current, clean Driving License. A5 PA1 and PA6 Chemical Spraying Certificates. A6 LANTRA qualifications in Chainsaw maintenance, cross-cutting and basic felling refresher and Wood chippers. A7 IOSH Working Safely (requirement to attain within 12 months). A8 Knowledge and understanding of safe working practices including manual handling and implementation of risk assessment corrective measures. A9 Good knowledge of short and long-term renovation procedures. A10 First Aid at Work Qualification (requirement to attain within 6 months). A11 Basic familiarity with standard office software such as MS Office, email and the internet. Desirable: B1 NVQ Level 2 Grounds Maintenance and Horticulture or equivalent. B2 Good knowledge and demonstrable experience of artificial sports surfaces. Skills Essential: C1 Strong, confident and adaptive communicator with excellent interpersonal skills; ability to start conversations as well as respond positively to enquires. C2 Ability to work under pressure, unsupervised and deal with customer queries effectively. C3 Work effectively as part of a team. C4 Ability to adapt and accommodate any situation that arises from last minute decision making to enhance the Universities reputation by delivering outstanding facilities. C5 Ability to meet and greet University club managers, Professional and Amateur football representatives, coaches or students listen to requests, problems or issues and using own initiative remedy all issues providing an excellent customer experience. C6 Excellent time management skills. C7 Ability to take initiative and innovate within the agreed dimensions and duties of the role. C8 Ability to organise workload and good problem-solving skills. C9 Able to operate, manoeuvre and maintain a wide variety of mechanical grounds maintenance equipment (ranging from tractor mounted equipment to pedestrian). C10 Ability to show compassion, respect to all users and have a sensitive approach to others using the site, to be helpful, considerate, and understanding. Desirable: D1 Ability to use defibrillator. D2 Ability to operate and manoeuvre mechanical grounds maintenance equipment. Experience Essential: E1 Relevant experience in an outdoor sports facility/golf course environment. Desirable: F1 Experience of successfully operating within a multi-functional team and delivering against agreed targets. F2 Learn new techniques and craft practices in line with evolving technology and legislative Health and Safety requirements.  This may involve attendance of training courses and conferences. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 4, £25,804 - £27,319 per annum pro rata, plus 6% shift allowance This post is part time (15 hours per week, worked Saturday - Sunday 08:30 - 16:00) and fixed term for up to 6 months from start date. Closing date: 23:45 9th October 2026 As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Grounds Operative Glasgow Posted 29 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Clinical Lecturer in Child Health Glasgow Clinical Academic Scale: £52,229 - £77,389 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-associate-6071392": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Associate Research Associate Back to jobs Research Associate Job details Posted 27 October 2026 Salary Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 209794 Expiry 10 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose You will contribute to/make a leading contribution to the project “Myelin plasticity in the adult and aged brain: A cross-species approach” working with Dr Cassandra Sampaio-Baptista, and group colleagues. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of research publications and research proposals as well as help manage and direct this complex and challenging project/disciplinary area as opportunities allow. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Take a leading role in the planning and conduct of assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the project deliverables and project/group/School/College research strategy. 2. Document research output including analysis and interpretation of all data, maintaining records and databases, drafting technical/progress reports and papers as appropriate. 3. Establish and maintain your research profile and reputation and that of the University of Glasgow/School/Research Group/area, including establishing and sustaining a track record of independent and joint publications of international quality, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 4. Survey the research literature and environment, understand the research challenges associated with the project & subject area, and develop/implement a suitable research strategy to deliver project/area requirements and opportunities. 5. Presentation of work at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance our research and/or impact profile. 6. Take a leading role in the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research and impact. 7. Take a leading role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues across the research group/area/School/College/University and wider community (e.g. academic and industrial partners). 8. Take a leading role in team/group meetings/seminars/workshops and School research group/area activities to enhance the wider knowledge, outputs and culture of the School/College. 9. Take the lead in the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 10. Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including budgets/expenditure. 11. Contribute as appropriate to teaching activities (e.g. demonstrating, etc) and associated administration, as may be assigned through the School in consultation with your line manager. 12. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 13. Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 14. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by your line manager and the Head of School. 15. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with University strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge & Qualifications Essential: A1 Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) plus track record of emerging independence within a research/professional environment, or alternatively possess professional qualifications and experience equivalent to PhD level plus the requisite experience in Cellular Neuroscience Skills Essential: C1 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within cellular Neuroscience and glia biology C2 Well-developed working knowledge of brain circuit mapping (e.g. viral vectors, optogenetics, etc.) C3 Excellent and extensive technical expertise in mouse stereotaxic surgeries C4 Ability to communicate material of a specialist or highly technical nature and to be effective both orally and in writing at a level appropriate to the activity. C5 Effective organisation skills to manage time, prioritise and deliver appropriate research, teaching and administrative responsibilities. Experience Essential: E1 Experience with in vivo electrophysiology E2 Evidence of an emerging track record of publications in a relevant Neuroscience field, evidenced as peer-reviewed articles in high quality journals and / or articles available as preprints compatible with enhancing the School’s research profile in accordance with School objectives. E3 Proven commitment to supporting the career development of colleagues and to other forms of collegiality appropriate to the career stage, including some experience of research project supervision. E4 Commitment to open research, as appropriate to the discipline, through open data, open code, open educational resources and practices that support replication. Desirable: F1 Experience in managing (transgenic) mouse colonies. F2 Extensive experience in immunohistochemistry and confocal microscopy F3 Experience in rodent behavioural testing F4 Experience with ex vivo electrophysiology F5 Experience in supervising or co-supervising undergraduate, Msc students or PhD students. F6 Experience of making distinct contributions to identifying and pursuing research funding (or equivalent business development) applications. Informal Enquiries should be directed to Dr Cassandra Sampaio-Baptista Cassandra.SampaioBaptista@glasgow.ac.uk ​ Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time, and has funding for up to 2 years The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date: 10 November 2026 at 23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Associate Glasgow Posted about 10 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Receptionist Glasgow £25,804 pa - £27,319 pa Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow Deputy Director of Procurement Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-assistant-6071984": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Research Assistant · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Assistant Research Assistant Back to jobs Research Assistant Job details Posted 28 October 2026 Salary Grade 6 £33,951-£37,694 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210335 Expiry 25 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose You will contribute to the CRUK-funded programme REMIT, working with Professor Daniel J. Murphy and the collaborating teams of IAMMED-Meso, REMIT and PREDICT-Meso, to investigate the mechanistic biological underpinnings and identify new therapeutic strategies for treatment of Mesothelioma. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of research publications and research proposals as well as help manage and direct this complex and challenging project as opportunities allow. Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with and under the guidance of the Principal/Co Investigator: 1.  Plan and conduct assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the project deliverables and Murphy lab/School of Cancer Sciences/College research strategy. 2.  Document research output including analysis and interpretation of all data, maintaining records and databases, drafting technical/progress reports and papers as appropriate. 3.  Develop and enhance your research profile and reputation and that of The University of Glasgow/School/Research Group, including contributing to publications of international quality in high profile/quality refereed publications, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 4.  Contribute to the presentation of work at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance our research profile. 5.  Contribute to the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research. 6.   Collaborate with colleagues and participate in team/ group/ meetings/ seminars/ workshops across the research Group/School/College/University and wider community. 7.   Contribute to the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 8.   Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including Budgets/Expenditure. 9.   Carry out modest Teaching activities (e.g. demonstrating etc) and associated admin as assigned by the Head of School and in consultation with Principal Investigator. 10.  Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in cancer research. 11.  Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 12.  Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School/Director of Research Institute. 13.  Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University Strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 SCQF Level 10 (Honours degree) in a relevant subject or a cognate discipline, or equivalent A2 Specialist theoretical and practical knowledge of Mesothelioma A3  A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of cancer biology A4  A Home Office Personal Licence for animal research in good standing Desirable: B1 An awarded, recently submitted or near completion, PhD in subject specialism or equivalent Skills Essential: C1 Demonstrable knowledge of signal transduction in Mesothelioma and proficiency in a range of relevant molecular biology techniques. C2  Research creativity and cross-discipline collaborative ability as appropriate C3  Excellent communication skills (oral and written), including public presentations and ability to communicate complex data/concepts clearly and concisely C4  Excellent interpersonal skills including team working and a collegiate approach C5  Appropriate workload/time/project/budget/people management skills C6  Extensive IT and data analysis/interpretation skills as appropriate C7  Self-motivation, initiative and independent thought/working C8  Problem solving skills including a flexible and pragmatic approach Experience Essential: E1  Sufficient relevant research experience (or equivalent) appropriate to an early career researcher E2  Experience working with genetically altered mouse models of Mesothelioma E3  Demonstrable experience in interpreting ultrasonography images, particularly for the detection and assessment of mesothelioma in mice. E4  Experience of scientific writing, as appropriate for an early career researcher E5  Proven ability to deliver accurate, high-quality, outputs in a timely and efficient manner E6  Evidence of an emerging track record of high-quality research publications and/or presentation at international conferences in cancer research ​ For enquiries regarding this post please contact Professor Daniel J. Murphy, Daniel.Murphy@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum. This post is full time, and has funding for up to 15 months The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date: 25 November 2026 @23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Assistant Glasgow Posted about 17 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951-£37,694 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-associate-6071947": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Associate Research Associate Back to jobs Research Associate Job details Posted 28 October 2026 Salary Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum End date 18 November 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210893 Expiry 18 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose We have an exciting opportunity for a Research Associate to make a leading contribution to the NERC-funded project in the Changing the Environment Strategic Programme Glasgow as a Living Lab Accelerating Novel Transformation (GALLANT) (P-I Professor Jaime Toney), Work Package 1, ‘Flood Adaptation and Creation of Riverscapes’ (Work Package), working with Work Package 1 Co-Leads/Co-Investigators (Dr Martin Hurst, Dr Gierdre Jokubauskaite, Dr Lazaros Kariolatas, and Professor Larissa Naylor). Our work package is interdisciplinary in scope, and the currently advertised role aims to produce new thinking and transdisciplinary research outputs to facilitate implementation of climate change adaptation actions to live well with growing tidal flood risks in Glasgow City Region. The successful candidate will lead a focussed piece of qualitative social science research examining decision-making and transformative governance arrangements that support implementation of climate change adaptation actions. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of a research publication, as well as engaging with stakeholders. The post involves engaging with multiple disciplines including geosciences, geomorphology, environmental economics, law and social sciences, and arts and humanities-based methods for creative engagement around climate futures. The tasks involved in this role build on the data gathered and research conducted by other members of the work package, so ability to work in a team, and willingness to be part of a collaborative, interdisciplinary research agenda is essential. Please contact Professor Jaime Toney (email: jaime.toney@glasgow.ac.uk), Dr Lazaros Kariolitas (email: lazaros.kariolitas@glasgow.ac.uk) and/or Professor Larissa Naylor (email: larissa.naylor@glasgow.ac.uk) for further information on this project and position. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Take a leading role in the planning and conducting of assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the deliverables of the Work Package. In particular, the Research Associate will: a. Study, document and analyse lessons learned from innovative climate adaptation interventions (such as Granton waterfront and Seafield Masterplan in Edinburgh) using semi-structured interviews and other qualitative research methods. b. Analyse pre-existing interview data of practitioners in Glasgow City Region, and conduct additional interviews if it proves necessary, in order to identify effective and equitable transformative governance arrangements and pathways that can support climate adaptation and flood risk management decision-making. c. Building on the case studies and an in-depth understanding of the regional governance arrangements, draw links to higher levels of governance (UK, Scotland) to identify pathways for effective flood/erosion risk and adaptation. 2. Document research outputs including maintaining records and database, analysis, interpretation and open access sharing of all data. 3. Establish and maintain your research profile and reputation and that of the University of Glasgow and the Project, including leading the writing of a collaborative academic paper for an internationally recognised, high-profile/quality refereed journal, working with the WP1 team to enhance the sharing and impact of our research. 4. With support from the work package team understand the research challenges associated with the project and subject area, develop/implement a suitable, critically engaged interdisciplinary research strategy. 5. Presentation of work at an international and/or national conference, and if desired, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance your research profile. 6. Have a supporting role in developing and maintaining collaborations with stakeholders and colleagues across the Work Package and wider community (e.g. the GALLANT project, Academic and Industrial Partners). Participation in team/group meetings/seminars/workshops with stakeholders at different levels (Glasgow City Council, Scottish Environmental Protection Agency, etc.) and other research activities to enhance the wider knowledge, outputs and culture of the of the Work Package and the GALLANT project. 7. Perform administrative tasks related to their activities within the Work Package. 8. Make a contribution to teaching activities and associated admin as assigned by the Head of School. 9. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 10. Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 11. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Line Manager and the Leads of the Work Package. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge and Qualifications Essential: A1 Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) in a relevant social science academic discipline (e.g. Political Science, Anthropology, Environmental Law, Development Studies, Human Geography, Urban Studies, Planning, or other related fields), or alternatively possess the equivalent in professional qualifications and experience, with experience of personal development in a similar or related role(s). A2 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within the wider subject area or subject specialism of geography, politics, environmental law, planning urban studies, human dimensions of climate change and/or environmental management. A3 Ability to demonstrate a degree of independence as illustrated by identification of project objectives from assessment of the literature, design and drafting of papers. A4 A knowledge of environmental/climate/development/planning governance in Scotland/UK through relevant research and/or in practice. Skills Essential: C1  Expertise in mixed methods qualitative social science research, including gathering, coding and analysis of preconstructed and self-constructed data. C2  Proven ability to deliver quality outputs for academic and non-academic audiences in a timely and efficient manner. C3 Proven ability to deliver quality outputs in a timely and efficient manner. C4 A track record of presentation and publication of research results in quality journals/conferences commensurate with career stage. C5 Effective organisation skills to manage time, prioritise and deliver appropriate research, teaching and administrative responsibilities. Desirable: D1 Evidence of research specifically on transformative governance and/or climate change adaptation. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of working with non-academic stakeholders, e.g. conducting research involving practitioners across sectors and/or co-production of policy recommendations. E2 Experience working in interdisciplinary research teams (this could include interdisciplinary PhD supervisory teams). E3 Experience of conducting interdisciplinary qualitative research on the issues of environmental and/or climate governance. E4 Sufficient depth of relevant research experience, normally including sufficient doctoral or postdoctoral experience in a related field, appropriate to an early career researcher. Desirable: F1 Experience of making a leading contribution in academic activities and/or demonstrable experience of research leadership (e.g. coordinating research activities, sessions at conferences). F2 Ability to demonstrate a degree of independence as illustrated by identification of project objectives from assessment of the literature and drafting of papers. F3 Experience of writing policy briefs, guidance or similar outputs for non-academic audiences. F4 Experience of leading or co-leading workshops with external non-academic stakeholders. F5 Sufficient depth of relevant interdisciplinary research experience, normally including sufficient postdoctoral experience in a related field such as within urban studies, law, geography, human dimensions of climate change, politics or environmental management or another applicable field appropriate to an early career researcher. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week), and has funding available for up to one year in the first instance.   A starting date of January 2027 is envisaged, or as soon as possible afterwards. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK.  This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  18 November 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Associate Glasgow Posted about 18 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951-£37,694 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/finance-assistant-6056064": {
      "title": "Finance Assistant",
      "description": "Finance Assistant · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Finance Assistant Finance Assistant Back to jobs Finance Assistant Job details Posted 09 October 2026 Salary Grade 4, £25,804 - £27,319 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 210296 Expiry 18 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose Provide comprehensive financial administrative support across the finance operational services to university colleagues, students, suppliers and customers. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Process financial transactions accurately, including journals and invoices, ensuring adherence to financial requirements and controls, and delivering within team KPIs and SLAs. 2. Support the delivery of a strong financial control environment across the team, including adherence to financial guidelines, policies and procedures. 3. Provide clear advice and guidance to others in accordance with standard financial processes and financial guidelines. 4. Monitor, allocate and resolve general day-to-day enquires in a timely manner to ensure delivery of Service Excellence and in line with SLAs, escalating as appropriate. 5. Work collaboratively across all Finance Operations teams to aide delivery of service excellence to all customers, both internal and external. 6. Support the resolution of balance sheet or bank reconciliation items in a timely and accurate manner, to validate accuracy of the books and records. 7. Maintain and ensure integrity of data aligned to University, College, School and Financial systems and regulations. 8. Assist with the testing and implementation of systems and procedures to ensure the continuous improvement of the process and service delivery. 9. Contribute and support continuous improvement, as appropriate. 10. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by line manager or Finance Operations Leadership. 11. Engage in reasonable professional development activities as appropriate. 12. Contribute to a culture of service excellence and control to delivery against key performance indicators, service level agreements and finance strategies. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills in a similar role. Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 5 or 6 (National 5 or 6, Scottish Vocational Qualification level 2 or 3) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 Knowledge and awareness of financial sanctions, anti-money laundering guidelines, fraud prevention measures, for example know your payer / supplier / customer checks. A3 Knowledge of GDPR and information privacy regulations. Skills Essential: C1 Good interpersonal and communication skills, both written and verbal. C2 Initiative and judgement to resolve routine problems independently. C3 Good analytical and problem-solving skills. C4 Excellent attention to detail and ability to work with high degree of numerical and data accuracy. C5 Strong IT skills, including Microsoft packages and relevant financial systems. C6 Ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a team. C7 Effective planning, organising and prioritising skills with ability to adapt to changing priorities or requirements in a timely manner. C8 Strong customer service skills. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of maintaining financial and non-financial data. E2 Experience working within a financial environment. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 4, £25,804 - £27,319 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours p/w) and fixed term to cover maternity leave for up to 12 months or until the substantive postholder returns. Please Note: If you are a current employee of the University of Glasgow, please be advised that the University would consider an internal secondment opportunity for this post. University of Glasgow applicants should seek clearance for release for internal secondment from their line manager before making a formal application. Closing date: 18 November 2026 This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Finance Assistant Glasgow Posted 20 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951-£37,694 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Programme and Development Manager Glasgow Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Law Glasgow logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/lecturer-in-farm-animal-clinical-skills-6073376": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills",
      "description": "Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Back to jobs Featured Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Job details Posted 29 October 2026 Salary Grade 7: £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210713 Expiry 12 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description We have an exciting opportunity to appoint a Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills to support the delivery of teaching within the School of Biodiversity, One Health and Veterinary Medicine with a specific focus on veterinary clinical skills, (farm animal/mixed practice) including gross pathology and clinical reasoning and may include farm visits. Job Purpose The postholder duties and responsibilities will be to contribute to and lead teaching, assessment, feedback, and administrative processes associated with undergraduate students. You may also be involved in the delivery of undergraduate clinical training in individual animal medicine and surgery, routine herd/flock health management and herd/flock health problem solving investigations. This is a Post-doctoral role in Farm Animal Clinical Skills at the School of  Biodiversity, One Health and Veterinary Medicine. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. To deliver teaching of clinical and professional skills and clinical reasoning, with a focus on farm animals, in a variety of settings, e.g. lecture theatres, the clinical skills facility, teaching laboratories, the Scottish Centre for Production Animal Health and Food Safety (SCPAHFS), Cochno Farm and farms external to the university with which the University of Glasgow has working relationships. 2. To contribute to production animal pathology and VPH teaching as appropriate and particularly in the clinical phase of the BVMS programme and be available for the supervision of under/post graduate research projects where appropriate. 3 To ensure appropriate communication between the clinical skills and farm animals teams with regard to current best practice. 4. To take responsibility for the delivery of a range of assessment activities and engage with the examination process as appropriate and provide effective, timely and appropriate feedback to students to support learning. 5. To contribute to the ongoing development and design of the curriculum, in a manner that supports a research led and scholarly approach to student learning. 6. To undertake administrative duties associated with educational leadership (e.g. module/course/portfolio) as requested by the Head of Division and to lead specific initiatives to enhance the learning experience and ensure the requirements of regulatory bodies are met. 7. To participate in quality assurance and enhancement activities, curriculum review and programme re-accreditation with the University and professional bodies to ensure high quality education provision is maintained. 8. To support the development of creative and innovative approaches to teaching and learning, in accordance with the University’s Quality Enhancement Strategy, so that the educational opportunities available to students are maintained or improved. 9. To participate in the Advisor of Studies (student mentorship) and Portfolio adviser Programmes and assist students with difficulties. 10. To contribute to the enhancement of the University's international profile in line with the Strategic Plan Knowledge/Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Veterinary degree, registered with the RCVS before commencing employment. Desirable: B1 Further professional veterinary qualifications in relevant production animal health or a related area. B2 Fellow of the Higher Education Academy. Skills Essential: C1 Clinical competence in individual animal and herd / flock level production animal/mixed veterinary medicine, pathology and surgery. C2 Excellent communication skills, both orally and written. C3 Enthusiasm for teaching and learning. C4 Excellent interpersonal skills and presentation skills. C5 Time, project and people management skills. C6 Ability to supervise and direct staff/students in the support of activities. C7 Ability to work independently and as part of a team. C8 Self-motivated, able to plan for contingencies and think strategically. C9 Ability to accept collegiate responsibilities and act accordingly. C10 Must have held a full, valid UK driving licence for 2 years. All non-UK applicants from the EU/EEA or Designated Countries are required to register with the DVLA and apply for a Driving Licence Counterpart (known as a D9). Special rules apply for drivers from Non Designated Countries. Please seek advice before applying. C11 Understanding and experience of IT applications/packages. Desirable: D1 Evidence of scholarship. D2 Understanding and experience of databases. Experience Essential: E1 Substantive experience of a clinical service with a substantial production animal case/mixed practice load. Desirable: F1 Experience in the delivery of teaching and practical skill training to undergraduate veterinary students (or equivalent). Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and open ended. The successful candidate for this post will be enrolled onto the University’s Early Career Development Programme (ECDP). This will provide you as an early career academic staff member to be developed and supported over a specified timeframe to facilitate the advancement of your academic career.  Information on the programme can be found here . All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal Enquiries should be directed to Professor Jenny Hammond, Jennifer.Hammond@glasgow.ac.uk and Dr Zamantha Marshall, Zamantha.Marshall@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 12th November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Glasgow Posted about 12 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Glasgow Unremunerated, however University will meet all reasonable expenses incurred in connection with membership of the Court or activities on behalf of the University, including childcare costs. Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Glasgow Grade 7: £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Technician Glasgow Grade 5: £28,031 - £31,236 per annum pro rata Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951-£37,694 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/external-member-appointment-to-the-information-policy-and-strategy-committee-6073668": {
      "title": "External Member appointment to the Information · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "External Member appointment to the Information · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Back to jobs External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Job details Posted 29 October 2026 Salary Unremunerated, however University will meet all reasonable expenses incurred in connection with membership of the Court or activities on behalf of the University, including childcare costs. Location Glasgow Job Type Court Appointment Reference 211133 Expiry 26 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee (Unremunerated position) Are you looking for a new and exciting opportunity to guide an ambitious, global university? The University is looking to appoint two IT professionals with particular expertise in Information and Cyber Security, and a strong commitment to Higher Education, as external members of its Information Policy and Strategy Committee , which is a Committee of the University Court (the governing body). This is a unique opportunity to become a critical friend, provide expert guidance and assist in taking the University forward. Ideally, candidates will demonstrate a range of skills and have significant strategic leadership experience gained within large, complex organisations. We would particularly welcome applications from individuals who have senior-level IT experience and expertise in Artificial Intelligence, digital innovation, or information and cyber security, who can demonstrate a commitment to the University of Glasgow's Glasgow 2036 vision, and who possess the governance knowledge, interpersonal skills, integrity, credibility, and communication abilities needed to provide effective challenge and support at Board level. Glasgow is one of the world's top 100 universities and is a member of the prestigious Russell Group of leading UK research universities.  It has more than 33,000 undergraduate and postgraduate students, an annual turnover of more than £1 billion, including research income of over £240m, and welcomes students from more than 140 countries worldwide, with excellent ratings for student satisfaction.  The successful candidate will be contributing to the University at an exciting time, with major capital projects under way and under consideration as part of the redevelopment of an enlarged campus; and the development of the new institutional strategic plan. Candidates will need to be able to commit to between 5 and 8 half-days per year to the duties. The position is non-executive and unremunerated, but expenses will be paid, including travel, child/dependent-care and lost earnings.   The appointment will be for four years in the first instance.  Candidates must not be employees or students of the University of Glasgow. The University of Glasgow is committed to equality and ensuring all students, staff and visitors have a positive experience of the learning, teaching, research and work environment. We are keen to hear from people excited by the opportunity to add value irrespective of their sex, gender identity, gender re-assignment, age, disability, sexual orientation, religion or belief, race and traditionally under-represented groups, and to preserve gender balance on the Court in accordance with the Gender Representation on Public Boards (Scotland) Act 2018, we would welcome and encourage applications from women. If you share our values and can help the University achieve its ambitious goals and deliver positive change, we would be delighted to hear from you. Individuals with relevant experience may contact the Professor Iain Gordon (Senior-Vice Principal & Deputy Vice Chancellor - Academic) Iain.Gordon@glasgow.ac.uk to discuss the position on an informal basis. Details about the University’s governing body and its committees are available from https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/governance/corporategovernance/ Applications should include a C.V. and a cover letter Closing Date : 23:45 on Thursday 26th November 2026 Interviews : Due to take place December 2026 Apply Now Save this job External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Glasgow Posted about 9 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Glasgow Unremunerated, however University will meet all reasonable expenses incurred in connection with membership of the Court or activities on behalf of the University, including childcare costs. Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Glasgow Grade 7: £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Technician Glasgow Grade 5: £28,031 - £31,236 per annum pro rata Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951-£37,694 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-technician-6073256": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "Research Technician · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Technician Research Technician Back to jobs Research Technician Job details Posted 29 October 2026 Salary Grade 5: £28,031 - £31,236 per annum pro rata Location Glasgow Reference 210733 Expiry 05 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Please note this vacancy is open to current University of Glasgow employees only. The School of Infection and Immunity is seeking an experienced Research Technician to support research activities in molecular microbiology and bacterial genetics under the supervision of Dr Arianne Babina (Principal Investigator). The Babina Lab uses microbiology, molecular genetics, biochemistry, experimental evolution, and large-scale “-omics” approaches to investigate how novel bacterial gene regulatory networks emerge and evolve to help bacteria rapidly adapt to environmental challenges, with a focus on antimicrobial resistance and pathogen evolution. Job Purpose The postholder will provide technical expertise in molecular microbiology and bacterial genetics, contributing to wet and dry lab experiments, lab management, and supervision and training of team members to support ongoing research. They will contribute to short-term research projects and collaborate as part of larger research teams. Additionally, they will perform a range of technical support activities aligned with the School’s research strategy. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Provide technical and research support for ongoing wet and dry research projects in the lab. 2. Undertake computational analysis of experimental datasets to support research objectives. 3. In liaison with the PI, advise, implement and evaluate improvements to existing protocols relevant as required. 4. Collect, analyse and interpret data using standard experimental and computational methods and ensure that accurate research records are kept, and databases are updated accordingly. 5. Plan and organise day to day work to ensure that the experimental goals are delivered within previously agreed timescales. 6. To supervise junior staff, where appropriate, and instruct, train and provide guidance to research staff and students in the techniques and operation of equipment and apparatus as directed by the supervisor, lab manager or PI. 7. Contribute to the well organised functioning of the laboratory by assisting the PI in maintaining equipment, managing stock levels, and purchasing consumables and supplies within budget, in coordination with other group members. 8. In consultation with the PI and floor coordinator, ensure the safe and efficient functioning of the laboratory area and facilities. This includes, but is not limited to, supporting the completion of risk assessments and COSHH assessments, ensuring that all Health and Safety paperwork is completed timeously and recorded accurately, and implementing and adhering to health and safety policies, as determined by the University and the Health and Safety Executive. 9. Collaborate with colleagues and participate in team meetings/discussions and departmental research group activities. 10. Keep up to date with advancements in technology and methodology, continually updating technical knowledge. 11. Be aware of and comply with Equality and Diversity policies of the University of Glasgow. The above duties are not exhaustive, and the post-holder will be required to undertake any other relevant duties as required by the PI/line manager which are commensurate with the grade of the post. Knowledge/Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1. Scottish Credit and Qualifications Framework level 7 (Advanced Higher / HNC, SVQ 3), or equivalent in relevant science subject. A2. Detailed technical knowledge of molecular biology, microbiology, biochemistry, and bacterial genetics. A3. Up to date knowledge of relevant laboratory Health and Safety legislation. Desirable: B1. Scottish Credit and Qualifications Framework level 9 (BSc Degree, SVQ Level 4) or equivalent in relevant scientific discipline. B2. Background or experience in bioinformatics, computational biology, or related fields. B3. Knowledge of Equality and Diversity policy. Skills Essential: C1. Experience in practical laboratory techniques in molecular biology and microbiology, including reagent and media preparation, sterile technique, bacterial culture and transformation, PCR, and molecular cloning. C2. Computer skills, including familiarity with Microsoft Office, relevant biological databases, and basic bioinformatics/webtools for exploring genomic and protein sequences and protein structures. C3. Demonstrable analytical and problem-solving abilities. C4. Proven interpersonal and communication skills. C5. Numeracy and literacy skills. C6. A high level of accuracy and attention to detail. C7. Excellent organisational and time management skills and an ability to prioritise workload to meet competing demands. C8. Use of initiative and judgement to resolve problems independently. C9. Ability to work individually and as part of a team. Desirable: D1. Some experience in analysing and interpreting biological or molecular data using computational tools. D2. Familiarity with a programming language used in biological data analysis, such as Python or R. Experience Essential: E1. Extensive relevant work experience (i.e. greater than 1 month) in a research laboratory setting. E2. Proficient in core molecular biology and microbiology techniques, including the preparation of reagents and media, as well as expertise in sterile technique, bacterial culture and transformation, PCR, and molecular cloning. E3. Experience using computer-based resources and biological databases to access and analyse biological information, such as genomic and protein sequences and protein structures. E4.  Demonstrated ability to rapidly learn, execute, troubleshoot, and adapt experimental protocols and/or computational workflows effectively. Desirable: F1. Demonstrated leadership experience, such as the ability to train and supervise others. F2. Familiarity with established bioinformatics pipelines and computational methods for biological data analysis. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum pro rata. This post is part time (28 hours per week) and fixed term up to 20th August 2027. This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries should be directed to Dr Arianne Babina, Arianne.Babina@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 5th November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Research Technician Glasgow Posted about 13 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Glasgow Unremunerated, however University will meet all reasonable expenses incurred in connection with membership of the Court or activities on behalf of the University, including childcare costs. Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Glasgow Grade 7: £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Technician Glasgow Grade 5: £28,031 - £31,236 per annum pro rata Research Assistant Glasgow Grade 6 £33,951-£37,694 per annum Research Associate Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
