    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-associate-in-intelligent-geophysics-for-offshore-wind-6074929": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind",
      "description": "Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind Back to jobs Featured Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind Job details Posted 30 October 2026 Salary Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum End date 29 November 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210874 Expiry 29 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose Applications are invited for the position of Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind at the James Watt School of Engineering. This position offers a unique opportunity to contribute to a major new UKRI-funded Fellowship at the intersection of artificial intelligence, scientific machine learning and subsurface engineering. The research will address fundamental challenges in the interpretation and integration of marine geophysical, geological and geotechnical data to improve subsurface characterisation and support decision-making throughout the development of offshore renewable energy projects. The successful candidate will play a leading role in the processing, interpretation and integration of geophysical datasets. The role will involve working with large and complex offshore datasets to develop improved understanding of geological variability, uncertainty and geohazards. Experience in artificial intelligence, machine learning or advanced data analytics is advantageous but not essential. The project will provide opportunities to build expertise in these areas and collaborate with a wider interdisciplinary team of engineers, geophysicists and data scientists exploring new AI-driven approaches to intelligent subsurface characterisation. Applications from candidates currently working in industry are encouraged. We welcome applicants seeking to bring expertise in offshore site characterisation, marine geophysics and subsurface interpretation into a research environment. Equivalent industrial experience will be considered alongside traditional academic career paths. The postholder will join an interdisciplinary team led by Dr Róisín Buckley as part of a UKRI-funded Fellowship. The role offers opportunities to collaborate with leading academic and industrial partners, including researchers across the UK's AI ecosystem in data science and artificial intelligence. The successful candidate will have access to world-class experimental and computational facilities and will play a key role in shaping an ambitious research programme at the interface of geophysics, engineering and AI. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Take a leading role in the planning and conduct of assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the project deliverables and project/group/School/College research strategy. 2. Document research output including analysis and interpretation of all data, maintaining records and databases, drafting technical/progress reports and papers as appropriate. 3. Establish and maintain your research profile and reputation and that of the University of Glasgow/School/Research Group/area, including establishing and sustaining a track record of independent and joint publications of international quality, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 4. Survey the research literature and environment, understand the research challenges associated with the project & subject area, and develop/implement a suitable research strategy to deliver project/area requirements and opportunities. 5. Presentation of work at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance our research and/or impact profile. 6. Take a leading role in the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research and impact. 7. Take a leading role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues across the research group/area/School/College/University and wider community (e.g. academic and industrial partners). 8. Take a leading role in team/group meetings/seminars/workshops and School research group/area activities to enhance the wider knowledge, outputs and culture of the School/College. 9. Take the lead in the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 10. Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including budgets/expenditure. 11. Contribute as appropriate to teaching activities (e.g. demonstrating, etc) and associated administration, as may be assigned through the School in consultation with your line manager. 12. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 13. Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 14. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by your line manager and the Head of School. 15. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with University strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1  Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) in geophysics, geoscience or a related discipline plus track record of emerging independence within a research/professional environment or alternatively possess professional qualifications and experience equivalent to PhD level plus the requisite experience. A2 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within the relevant research disciplines as outlined in the Job Purpose and demonstrable expertise in the interpretation of geophysical data. A3 Well-developed working knowledge of research methods, technical models, equipment and techniques relevant to the project/discipline as outlined in the Job Purpose. Ability to manage, integrate and interpret geophysical datasets. Skills Essential: C1 Ability to communicate material of a specialist or highly technical nature and to be effective both orally and in writing at a level appropriate to the activity. C2  Developing track record of research outputs compatible with enhancing the School’s research profile in accordance with School objectives. C3 Proven commitment to supporting the career development of colleagues and to other forms of collegiality appropriate to the career stage, including some experience of research project supervision. C4 Effective organisation skills to manage time, prioritise and deliver appropriate research, teaching and administrative responsibilities. C5 Commitment to open research, as appropriate to the discipline, through open data, open code, open educational resources and practices that support replication. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of making distinct contributions to identifying and pursuing research funding (or equivalent business development) applications. E2 Experience of planning and progressing work activities within professional guidelines or organisational policy, applying initiative and independent judgement. Desirable: F1  Demonstrable expertise in the interpretation, integration and geological understanding of marine geophysical data relevant to offshore site characterisation. F2 Knowledge of emerging applications of artificial intelligence, machine learning and advanced data analytics in the geosciences. F3 Experience of participating in activities that translate research into impact to benefit the economy and wider society. F4 Experience of contributing to the design and delivery of teaching and assessment in higher education or other relevant environments. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week), and has funding available until 30 September 2029 in the first instance. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  29 November 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind Glasgow Posted about 10 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition Glasgow Grade 8: £50,253 - £58,225 per annum MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead Glasgow Clinical Consultant scale: £111,430 - £148,064 per annum pro rata External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Glasgow Unremunerated, however University will meet all reasonable expenses incurred in connection with membership of the Court or activities on behalf of the University, including childcare costs. Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Glasgow Grade 7: £41,064 - £46,049 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-fellow-in-applied-ai-for-the-energy-transition-6074890": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition",
      "description": "Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition Back to jobs Featured Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition Job details Posted 30 October 2026 Salary Grade 8: £50,253 - £58,225 per annum End date 29 November 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210873 Expiry 29 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose Applications are invited for the position of Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition within the James Watt School of Engineering. This position offers a unique opportunity to contribute to a major new UKRI-funded Fellowship at the intersection of artificial intelligence, scientific machine learning and subsurface engineering. The research focuses on developing physics-informed machine learning approaches to improve understanding of the subsurface, a critical challenge in the deployment of offshore wind and other net-zero energy infrastructure. Prior domain experience in geotechnics, geophysics or offshore engineering is not required. We welcome applications from candidates in machine learning, artificial intelligence, computer science, statistics, data science, engineering, applied mathematics, physics or a closely related discipline. The successful candidate will undertake research in physics-informed machine learning, uncertainty quantification, predictive modelling and geospatial data integration to improve subsurface characterisation and support decision-making for offshore renewable energy developments. The research will create opportunities for methodological advances in machine learning and uncertainty-aware modelling while addressing important challenges in offshore renewable energy and the net-zero transition. The postholder will join an interdisciplinary team of engineers, data scientists and geophysicists led by Dr Róisín Buckley as part of a UKRI-funded Fellowship. The role offers opportunities to collaborate with leading academic and industrial partners, including researchers across the UK's AI ecosystem in data science and artificial intelligence. The successful candidate will have access to world-class experimental and computational facilities and will play a key role in shaping an ambitious research programme at the interface of geophysics, engineering and AI. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Play a leading role in the management and development of the project and to work independently and in collaboration to bring the project to a successful completion including establishing and maintaining external collaborations both nationally and internationally. 2. Play a leading role in the planning, design and conduct of research, individually or jointly, in accordance with the project deliverables and project/Institute/College/University research strategy. 3. Establish and maintain an extensive research profile and reputation and that of the University of Glasgow/ School/ Research Group, sustaining a track record of independent and joint publications of international quality in high profile/quality refereed publications, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 4. Play a leading role in the presentation of research findings at high profile national and international conferences, seminars and meetings to enhance the research profile of the project/Institute/College/University. 5. Lead the development, both independently and in collaboration with others (including stakeholders) of the identification of potential funding sources and lead in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research of international quality. 6. Take a leading role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues across the project and in the broader Institute/College/University and wider community (e.g. academic and other collaborators/partners). 7. Play a leading role in the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 8. Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including Budgets/Expenditure. 9. Supervise Masters students and act as co-supervisor to PhD students as assigned by the Director of Institute and/or senior group leader. 10. Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the project and Institute, including budgets/expenditure. 11. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline in order to provide advice and guidance to students and less experienced colleagues. 12. Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 13. Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School/Director of Research Institute. 14. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University Strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) in machine learning, artificial intelligence, computer science, statistics, data science, engineering, applied mathematics, physics or a closely related discipline, plus track record of emerging independence within a research/professional environment or alternatively possess professional qualifications and experience equivalent to PhD level plus the requisite experience. A2 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues, as outlined in the Job Purpose, and future directions within the wider subject area or subject specialism. A3 Specialist theoretical and practical knowledge of research methods, technical models, and techniques relevant to the project/discipline as outlined in the Job Purpose. Skills Essential: C1 Excellent scientific programming skills, and experience with modern machine-learning frameworks. C2 An established track record of presentation and publication of research results in quality publications. C3 Commitment to open research, as appropriate to the discipline, through open data, open code, open educational resources and practices that support replication. C4  Proven commitment to supporting career development of colleagues and to other forms of collegiality appropriate to the career stage. Experience Essential: E1 Experience developing and applying machine-learning methods to scientific, engineering, environmental, geospatial, or physical-system datasets. E2 Experience of delivering quality outputs in a timely and efficient manner. E3 Experience of independence as illustrated by identification of project objectives from assessment of literature, design and analysis of research data and drafting of papers. E4 Sufficient depth of relevant research experience in a relevant or related field, appropriate to an early career researcher. Desirable: F1 Experience managing, processing and analysing large and complex datasets, including development of reproducible computational workflows. F2 Experience working within interdisciplinary research teams spanning computational and domain sciences. F3 Experience of participating in activities that translate research into impact to benefit the economy and wider society. F4 Experience of contributing to the design and delivery of teaching and assessment in higher education or other relevant environments. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 8, £50,253 - £58,225 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week), and has funding available until 30 September 2029 in the first instance. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  29 November 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition Glasgow Posted about 11 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition Glasgow Grade 8: £50,253 - £58,225 per annum MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead Glasgow Clinical Consultant scale: £111,430 - £148,064 per annum pro rata External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Glasgow Unremunerated, however University will meet all reasonable expenses incurred in connection with membership of the Court or activities on behalf of the University, including childcare costs. Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Glasgow Grade 7: £41,064 - £46,049 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/mbchb-clinical-assessment-lead-6074665": {
      "title": "MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead",
      "description": "MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead Back to jobs MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead Job details Posted 30 October 2026 Salary Clinical Consultant scale: £111,430 - £148,064 per annum pro rata Location Glasgow Job Type Clinical Reference 211274 Expiry 16 Nov 2026 23:45 Job description We have an exciting opportunity for an MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead to join the Undergraduate Medical School within the School of Medicine, Dentistry & Nursing. This is a part-time appointment (1 PA, 4 hours per week) on a fixed-term basis for up to 36 months. In this role, you will provide strategic leadership for clinical assessment across the MBChB programme, with a particular focus on the development, delivery, and quality assurance of Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs). The School of Medicine, Dentistry & Nursing is internationally recognised for its innovative teaching methods, world-class facilities, and commitment to excellence in undergraduate and postgraduate education. As part of the College of Medical, Veterinary & Life Sciences, the School offers a dynamic and collaborative environment in which to contribute to the education of future doctors. Job Purpose Responsibility for the provision of leadership in clinical assessment in the MBChB with a particular focus on the OSCE. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Provide leadership for clinical assessment, working in partnership with University and NHS colleagues to review, revise and develop the clinical assessment programme within the framework of: a. the requirements of the General Medical Council b. best educational practice c. the University of Glasgow Learning and Teaching Strategy. 2. Work with the wider assessment team to ensure clinical assessments are: a. secure, robust and discriminating and b. appropriately resourced, c. documented in a clear, accurate, accessible manner and reviewed annually d. Comply with the deadlines required to deliver the assessment schedule 3. Lead the development of assessment materials: liaise with Year, Block and Vertical Theme Leads to align their content with the MB ChB programme as a whole, ensure their timeous delivery to the appropriate University offices and/or systems, undertake the final sign-off of all stations on the relevant platform and complete the assessment blueprints. 4. Lead training, briefing and calibration sessions for simulated patients, actors and examiners for all MB ChB OSCE exams (at present, 6 diets per year), and liaise with external examiners throughout the assessment process. 5. Lead the assessment team on exam days, in co-operation with colleagues. 6. Liaise with academic colleagues in the analysis and presentation of examination results, and present the relevant data to the OSCE Exam Boards. 7. Ensure that NHS staff involved in OSCEs are aware of University and UMS policies and practices in relation to post-exam feedback to students. 8. Provide post-exam feedback directly to students, in Year groups or individually (face to-face or online), and answer relevant email correspondence timeously. 9. Participate in the UMS Assessment Committee and marshal evidence to support any proposed change. 10. Participate in relevant national meetings, ensuring that the UMS is employing recognised good practice and complies with GMC requirements. Knowledge, Qualifications ,Skills & Experience Knowledge, Qualifications Essential: A1 Registrable qualification with the GMC. A2 Experience in clinical teaching and assessment. Desirable: B1 Certificate in Academic Practice or equivalent learning and teaching qualification. Skills Essential: C1 Familiarity with current UK processes and practice related to clinical examination and assessment. C2 Good administrative and organisational skills. C3 Good IT skills. C4 Ability to engage and work effectively with a range of colleagues in developing clinical assessment methodologies. C5 Excellent communication skills, both written and oral. C6 Excellent interpersonal skills with the ability to lead, negotiate and inspire others. C7 Willingness and ability to work as part of the wider UMS Team. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of supervising MB ChB students. E2 Experience of delivering undergraduate and/or postgraduate clinical assessments. Desirable: F1 Ability to deliver research-informed strategic overview of clinical assessment strategies and methodologies. F2 Experience of academic assessment of undergraduate and / or postgraduate students. F3 Use of technology to enhance teaching. F4 Familiarity with technological enhancements of OSCE delivery and analysis. Further Information Contact Dr Margaret McMillan, Interim Head of the UMS (margaret.mcmillan@glasgow.ac.uk) for informal discussion or further information For further information regarding Glasgow and the University: http://www.gla.ac.uk For further information regarding the College of Medical, Veterinary and Life Sciences: http://www.gla.ac.uk/colleges/mvls/. For further information regarding the School of Medicine, Dentistry and Nursing: http://www.gla.ac.uk/schools/medicine/ Terms and Conditions Salary will be £111,430 - £148,064 per annum pro rata of the Clinical Consultants scale. This is a part-time appointment (1 PA, 4 hours per week) on a fixed-term basis for up to 36 months. Contractual arrangements will be considered and agreed at the offer stage (e.g., if employed by the NHS, you may continue to hold your substantive role with the NHS and payments paid under a separate arrangement). This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . As part of Team UofG you will be a member of a world changing, inclusive community, which values ambition, excellence, integrity and curiosity. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries should be directed to Dr Margaret McMillan, Margaret.Mcmillan@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 16th November 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead Glasgow Posted about 13 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Intelligent Geophysics for Offshore Wind Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Fellow in Applied AI for the Energy Transition Glasgow Grade 8: £50,253 - £58,225 per annum MBChB Clinical Assessment Lead Glasgow Clinical Consultant scale: £111,430 - £148,064 per annum pro rata External Member appointment to the Information Policy and Strategy Committee Glasgow Unremunerated, however University will meet all reasonable expenses incurred in connection with membership of the Court or activities on behalf of the University, including childcare costs. Lecturer in Farm Animal Clinical Skills Glasgow Grade 7: £41,064 - £46,049 per annum logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    }
